1. e4 c6 { The bedrock of this repertoire is the Caro-Kann defence, which is reached after 1.e4 c6. } 2. d4 d5 { White has many options after 1. e4 c6, but the aim of black is always to play 2....d5, as in the diagram.
But the position here, as we will explore further, can also be reached after 1. d4 c6 2. e4 d5.
Playing c6 as a response to d4 thus enables black to potentially transpose into the Caro-Kann, which is otherwise not invited by 1.d4. } *

