{ If you Google 1.d4 c6, you will see several forum discussions in which posters are desperate to refute the value of this approach. It would appear that they are affronted by its unprincipled nature!

However, numerous strong players have confirmed that there is no way to punish 1....c6. Black has to accept that this is a perfectly sound opening. }
1. d4 { Furthermore, all lines here have been checked with Stockfish. Everything advocated here is, at the very least, playable, and virtually all lines are completely sound.
For example, after d4, Stockfish rates d5 and c6 as equally good, stating that white is +0.2 after either move. Stockfish believes that 1...c6 is also just as playable as the far more common transpositional attempt 1...e6. } 1... c6 { Yet according to the Chess365 masters database, 1...c6 has been played as a response to 1.d4 less than 300 times in over 236,000 serious games! By comparison, black has commenced with 1...e6 over 10,000 times.
This is a curious imbalance, and also imbues 1...c6 with a surprise quality, which can have some minor value, particularly in blitz chess. } *

