1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { Most commonly played in my experience is the Advance Variation. White, as the name would suggest(!), advances the e-pawn when it's challenged by the black d-pawn, and attempts to gain a space advantage. } 3... Bf5 { Black typically places his bishop outside of the pawn chain. Also possible is 3...c5, but 3...Bf5 is more solid, and can be considered the classical approach to this line.
Ideally, black wants to play e6 immediately, without blocking in the dark-squared bishop, thus creating a triangle of pawns in the centre of the board. This offers an eminently solid structure. } 4. h4 { White has many possible moves here, but it's important to be aware of the following trap. After h4, if 4...e6, the bishop is trapped by 5. g4. } (4. Nf3 e6 { White can also play more solidly here, but now black can complete the aforementioned pawn triangle. } 5. Bd3 (5. Be2 { The Short Variation of the Advanced system is commonly seen at high levels. White avoids having the bishop exchanged on d3, and hopes to gain a lead in development with a secure king position. A subtle approach, but one that can be countered. } 5... c5 { This instant c5 break is the principled response here. Ne7 is also playable, but this is simply the most logical move, immediately propositioning the white centre. } 6. c3 { c3 is usual here. Be3 has also been tried several times in my games, after which black can simply take on d4, with less central control for white resulting. } 6... cxd4 7. cxd4 Nc6 { Ne7 is another possible try here, but the text move seems more logical. } 8. O-O { Nc3 is also a strong riposte here, but it's more principled for white to castle in this system, emphasising the lead in development and ultra-safe king position. } 8... Nge7 9. Nbd2 { Black has basically equalised here, but still faces a conundrum in terms of piece development. } 9... Bg6 10. Nb3 Nf5 { This would be my choice, which places the light-squared bishop on a strong diagonal, the knight on the typical f5, and ensures that, without disruption, black is two moves away from castling } 11. Bg5 { This is white's best hope to play for a small advantage. } 11... Be7 12. Bxe7 { Black is slightly better after any other move. } 12... Qxe7 13. h3 O-O { White is perhaps slightly better here, hence the fact that this system has become a highly-regarded weapon at the highest level. However... } 14. Rc1 Rac8 { ....after this logical continuation, black should be okay, with its only problematical piece being the f5-knight. }) 5... Bxd3 6. Qxd3 { This Bd3 manouevre is often played in this line, and it's usually prudent for black to exchange the bishops. This is already a very solid position for black; the next priority is to complete development and castle. } 6... c5 { This is the most principled move here, but Ne7, Nd7, and h6 - all commonly played general moves in this opening - are also playable. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. O-O { A sharper try for white here is 8. c4, after which Ne7 is prudent. } 8... Qc7 9. Nc3 a6 { a6 is often a useful move in the Caro-Kann (not to mention other openings), prophylactically preventing the knight, bishop, or queen coming to b5 in many lines. } 10. Be3 Bxe3 11. Qxe3 Ne7 12. Rfe1 O-O { This would be a very promising position for black. Often in the Advanced variation, the e5 pawn can become weak if white missteps slightly. Black should now contest the c-file, with moves such as b5, Rc8, Nc6, or Nd7 > c5, or Nd7 > b6 > c4, while black's other knight often belongs on f5. } 13. a3 Nd7 14. Rac1 Rac8 15. Ne2 Nf5 16. Qf4 Nc5 { This is a pleasing position for black, with strong c-file control, no structural weaknesses, and long-term pressure against the e-pawn. }) (4. Nc3 e6 { To provide another illustrative example, a game that I recently played against a 2273 opponent began like this. } 5. g4 Bg6 6. Nge2 Nd7 7. h4 h6 8. h5 Bh7 9. Be3 c5 10. Qd2 cxd4 11. Bxd4 a6 12. O-O-O Bc5 13. f4 Qc7 14. Kb1 Ne7 15. Bh3 O-O-O { White was probably marginally better here; arguably better is Rc8 and castling kingside. But my position was decent, and I went on to win in 27 moves after white underestimated my c-file pressure (see: https://lichess.org/EXHIO4Dt) }) 4... h6 { Instead, black makes an escape square for the bishop with h6. Also playable is h5, but I prefer the less committal h6, and so does Stockfish. } 5. g4 Be4 6. f3 Bh7 { Now if 5. g4, black forces white to commit the advance of its f-pawn, before retreating to the sanctuary of h7. Other playable moves here are h5 and c3, after which black should complete the pawn triangle with e6. } 7. Bd3 (7. e6 { This move isn't seen often at beginner or even intermediate level. But it does appear on the board with more regularity, the higher that you climb the rating ladder. } 7... Nf6 { Black can take this pawn, but its pieces will be awfully cramped, and the light squares will perilously weak. Suddenly, the black position appears worryingly permeable.
Thus, more sensible is to play Nf6, but this also leads to sharp play. This is one of the most critical lines. } 8. Nc3 Qd6 9. exf7+ Kxf7 10. Bh3 { White has several possible ideas here. Although the king looks poorly placed on f7, it is, in fact, perfectly safe. But black must play with precision in order to survive. } (10. g5 hxg5 11. hxg5 Bxc2 12. Qxc2 Rxh1 { 10. g5 is a mistake here, allowing this neat combination. White has compensation for the lost material, but it shouldn't be enough to be equal with sound play from black. }) 10... Nbd7 { Stockfish states that the best move here is e5, but it's psychologically difficult for a human to make a pawn break in this position.
I would instead recomment Nbd7, after which black has complete equality after anything other than f4. } (10... e5 { More principled, though is to make the e5 break immediately. This seems scary with your king uncastled, but white is very undeveloped and has left significant space with pawn advances, so it is quite logical. } 11. g5 exd4 (11... Na6 { This innocuous move begins to turn the tables for black. This is a profoundly double-edged position, but black is doing quite well. } 12. dxe5 (12. Nge2 hxg5 13. hxg5 Bf5 14. dxe5 Qxe5 15. Bf4 Qe6 16. gxf6 Rxh3 17. Rxh3 Bxh3 18. fxg7 Bxg7 { Black is at least equal and probably slightly better after this razor sharp variation, in which white has to find several only moves. }) 12... Qxe5+ 13. Kf1 hxg5 14. hxg5 Bc5 { This is now a highly dangerous position for both sides! If anything, black's king appears slightly safer, but white can gain tempi by harassing the black queen. There are many possible moves here, but this is definitely playable for black. })) 11. f4 (11. g5 hxg5 12. hxg5 Bf5 13. Qd2 Bxh3 14. gxf6 exf6 { If white goes for a direct attack then black is actually quite comfortable here, and has some positional pluses in exchange for its weakened king position. }) 11... e6 { If 11. f4 then 11...e6 is essential. } 12. Nf3 Kg8 13. O-O Ne4 { Black is uncomfortable here, and must untangle its pieces, but white has no direct attack. This is undoubtedly one of the most complicated and challenging positions in the entire Caro-Kann, and one that you will very rarely encounter, particularly at beginner level. }) 7... Bxd3 8. Qxd3 e6 { White has two main moves here. The first is the relatively straightforward Bd3, after which black can snap this bishop off, and play for equality. 7. e6 is discussed above. } 9. f4 c5 { Black almost always plays for this c5 break in the Advanced variation. I've picked f4 here as it's the most direct plan, but white could also play h5, Ne2, c3, or even the dubious Nc3. Regardless, black must break with c5 immediately. } 10. Nf3 Nc6 11. dxc5 (11. h5 { h5 is also playable here, but black's position is quite comfortable after this effort, with a plethora of strong replies available. } 11... Nxd4 12. Nxd4 cxd4 { This seems the most natural riposte, and black is even marginally better after the natural Qxd4. Black can transfer the knight to c6 via e7, and can hope for a superior middlegame. } 13. Nd2 { Better for white is this calm knight development, as the d-pawn is going nowhere, and will soon be recaptured. } 13... Ne7 14. Nf3 Nc6 15. Bd2 Qb6 16. O-O-O { Nonetheless, black is very comfortable now, and has a choice of viable plans. Stockfish encourages the a5 push, in order to expose the slightly loose white king position, but the more positional Bc5, a6, and Rc8 are all possible. I would wish to keep queenside castling as an option here, though. } 16... Bc5 { So this is a conservative, but safe, choice. } 17. f5 O-O-O { Now black can castle queenside, having comfortably equalised. }) 11... Bxc5 12. h5 Nge7 13. a3 a6 14. b4 Ba7 { Thematically, the c5 break, knights coming to e7, d7, c6, but rarely f6, controlling the c-file, and moves such as c5, Nc5, Rc8, etc, with this aim, are all important in the Advance Variation. We will also encounter them frequently in other Caro-Kann lines, and in our d4 repertoire. } 15. Nc3 Qb6 { Black should be appreciative of this position, and should aim to castle kingside imminently. Rc8 is also a strong move, and common idea, here. Controlling the c-file is critical in the Advance variation, and for that matter in the Caro-Kann generally. Often the c4 and c3 squares can be liabilities for white, and valuable outposts for black. } *

