1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 { The classical Caro-Kann begins with these moves. Also played is 3. Nd2, but this just transposes to exactly the same position in this repertoire. } 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 (4. f3 { One sideline that merits brief consideration is the Blackmar–Diemer Gambit. This can undoubtedly be a weapon in amateur chess, but also should be completely refutable. } 4... exf3 { I wouldn't even consider not taking the pawn. Nf6 is also playable. } 5. Nxf3 Nf6 { Black can also pin the bishop with Bg4, but I see no reason to immediately hand white the privilege of a sharper position. } 6. Bd3 (6. h3 Bf5 { If h3 here then the bishop can come to f5. }) 6... Bg4 { On the other hand, this now seems a sensible scheme for the always prickly prospect of light-squared bishop development. } 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Qxd4 { There are many possible moves for white after Bg4, but this is a critical line. Black wins another pawn on d4, but now white has a huge lead in development. } 9. Be3 Qh4+ { White could go back with the queen, go straight to b4, or pin the bishop via e5, and still be better. But this is the most promising approach. } 10. g3 Qb4 11. O-O-O { Black is definitely better here, but, considering the lead in development, a gargantuan white attack could materialise in little time if black goes awry over the next few moves. } 11... Nbd7 12. g4 { There are so many possibilities for white here that it's impossible to predict how a human opponent would play, but this seems the most forcing variation. } 12... h6 { Stockfish favours e6 here, but that requires a tactical melee in order to work. This approach is far more prudent. } 13. Rhf1 e6 { Black has been better throughout this sequence, and if we can castle then we can see a superior position on the horizon. } 14. Be2 { Actually, there is little that white can do here in order to cause black problems, and this move, which opens up the rook on the d-file, appears the best try. } 14... Be7 15. h4 O-O-O 16. Bxa7 { Not the best move, but probably the most likely human response to the position. } 16... Nc5 { Black is much better now, and would also be better after Bc5. }) (4. Bc4 { Another possible gambit is the Von-Hennig Gambit. White unsoundly jettisons the e-pawn for rapid development and attacking potential. } 4... Nf6 5. Nge2 (5. f3 { Another idea that I've seen here is this double-gambit. White gets a big lead in development, but black should be fine with correct play. } 5... exf3 6. Nxf3 Bf5 7. Bg5 e6 8. Nh4 Bg6 9. Nxg6 hxg6 10. Qd3 Nbd7 11. h4 Be7 12. O-O-O { Black is better, but must still tread warily. } 12... Nb6 13. Bb3 Nbd5 { Other options are available, but this approach is supremely solid. } 14. Bxf6 Nxf6 15. g4 Qc7 16. g5 Nh5 17. Ne4 O-O-O { White definitely has compensation for the pawn. But not enough! }) 5... Bf5 { Stockfish likes b5 here, but I have always played this way, and this is Stockfish's second choice. } 6. Ng3 { Castling is also possible, but this played by humans almost without exception. } 6... Bg6 { Again, I'm going against Stockfish here. The Silicon behemoth prefers e6 here, but this seems far more natural. } 7. O-O b5 { e6 is also possible here, but I believe that this queenside expansion is preferable. } 8. Bb3 a5 9. a3 a4 10. Ba2 e6 11. Re1 Nbd7 12. Ncxe4 { White has won back the pawn, but black is well-developed, has a strong position, and can be considered slightly better. } 12... Be7 13. Nxf6+ Nxf6 14. Bg5 O-O { This is a rock solid position for any regular Caro-Kann player. }) 4... Bf5 { I favour the traditional approach with Bf5 here. Nd7 and Nf6 are also played with regularity. } 5. Ng3 { White can play Nc5, but this line doesn't merit too much investigation, as it's rarely played. Also playable is Qf3, after which black has a comfortable game following e6.
I would estimate, though, that Ng3 is played 95% of the time, and this tallies with the Chessgames.com opening database. } (5. Bd3 { This is another possible gambit in the Classical Caro-Kann, which seemingly hands the d-pawn. } 5... Qxd4 { However, this is one of the more playable gambit lines in the Caro-Kann, in my humble opinion. White gets good compensation for the d-pawn. } (5... Bxe4 6. Bxe4 Nf6 7. Bf3 Nbd7 { Also possible here is this solid line, although it's not particularly principled to swap bishop for knight in this fashion. }) 6. Nf3 Qd8 { Black doesn't have a lot of choice here. } 7. Qe2 e6 8. Bf4 Nd7 9. O-O Ngf6 10. Rad1 Bxe4 11. Bxe4 Qb6 12. Nd2 { Black has definitely equalised here, and has a pleasant choice, including capturing the b-pawn, but white has a significant space advantage and can be reasonably satisfied with this position. Nonetheless, black has nothing to fear here. }) 5... Bg6 6. h4 (6. Nf3 Nd7 7. Nh4 (7. Bc4 { Black can also deviate with Bc4 here. } 7... e6 { A natural response to this is e6, and white will often castle kingside. } 8. O-O Ngf6 9. Ng5 { While this line can be played positionally, white can also arrange a sacrifice on e6. This can be tricky to face, as play becomes particularly sharp, and black can easily deviate from the appropriate path. } 9... Be7 { h6 is best here, but in a game I played I made the mistake of trying the quiet Be7, and the explosive sacrifice from white was detonated immediately. Black was worse, but I managed to wriggle out and win. You can see this game with my 2318-rated opponent here: https://lichess.org/DSK6LO7R1Ukj }) 7... e6 8. Nxg6 hxg6 { Another idea here is to simply exchange the light-squared bishop, as indicated in this line. Black doesn't need to fear this whatsoever, but it can sometimes necessitate queenside castling. } 9. Qd3 Ngf6 10. Be2 Qc7 11. O-O Bd6 12. c4 O-O-O { Black has completed development, its pieces are superbly placed, and black also has excellent compensation for the superficially wounded pawn structure via the open h-file. White has really achieved nothing of note in the opening here. }) 6... h6 { As in the Advance Variation, black has to be careful here to ensure that the light-squared bishop isn't trapped. } 7. h5 Bh7 8. Nf3 Nf6 9. Bd3 (9. Bc4 e6 10. Bf4 Be7 11. O-O O-O { Bc4 is sometimes chosen here by white players, but it's not particularly impressive. Black can complete development in unflustered fashion, and can ultimately claim a slightly better position. }) 9... Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 { There are many options here for white, but common ideas are to push the pawn to h5, swap the bishop off with Bd3, and develop the knight normally.
White will also sometimes advance one of the knights and attack the light-squared bishop. This can be allowed or avoided, depending on whether it would irreparably damage black's pawn structure. } 11. Bd2 { Bf4 is also possible here, which can be considered an open invitation to play Qa5+. This doesn't result in an a tangible advantage, but the black side is undoubtedly well-placed. } 11... Be7 12. O-O-O { White often castles queenside in this line. This can lead to double-edged play, but principally speaking the white king is perfectly safe here. } 12... O-O { Black is fundamentally sound. Usually, the black knight on b8 will come to d7. c5 is a typical idea in this structure. } 13. Ne5 Nbd7 { White will often put a knight on e5, even though Stockfish states that it's not always the best move. } 14. f4 c5 { Now we can play the c5 break, and the middle game is underway. } *

