1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 { With the Exchange Variation, white aims to play for a small positional edge. Again, there are several different approaches here, but I'm going to primarily focus on a setup that has been utilised by both Magnus Carlsen and Fabiano Caruana as white, while the legendary Bobby Fischer was also a pioneer of this line. On reflection, there is no quibbling with the pedigree of this system! } 4. Bd3 { The idea behind this move is simple and quite neat - deprive the light-squared bishop of the f5 square. Black must therefore implement a different scheme of development when facing this line. } 4... Nf6 5. c3 { Stockfish prefers Nf3 here, but this quiet pawn push has found favour with both the world number 1 and world number 2 in classical chess.
Either way there is only one logical response for black, even though other moves are playable. } 5... Bg4 { This solves black's problems with the placement of the light-squared bishop. } 6. Qb3 { Nf3 is also playable here, but I want to highlight one important tactic in this position. } 6... Qc7 7. h3 { Not 7. Bf4??, as indicated in the text below. } (7. Bf4 Qxf4 8. Qxb7 { Bf4 now looks tempting, and it seems as if white will win the exchange by capturing the a8 rook, which cannot be prevented. However, sadly for white... } 8... Qc1# { Qc1 is mate! In short, the Bf4 idea doesn't work, so black can afford to airily administer this simple queen move. }) 7... Bh5 8. Ne2 (8. Nd2 { In a win that I recently had against a 2299-rated player, my opponent chose Nd2 here. Play continued as follows. } 8... e6 9. Ngf3 Bd6 10. a4 Nbd7 11. a5 O-O { It was perfectly fine for black to castle kingside here; I was quite satisfied with my position at this point. } 12. Qc2 a6 13. O-O Rac8 14. Re1 Nb8 { I quite liked this rerouting of the knight, but Stockfish frigidly proclaims that simply centralising the other rook is better. } 15. Ne5 Nc6 16. Nxc6 bxc6 17. Nf1 Bg6 18. Bg5 { This move was a mistake, allowing my natural looking response, which gives black a nagging positional edge. } 18... Ne4 19. Be3 Bf4 { This seemed a natural way to potentially bring my queen into an attack on the kingside, but computers hate this move! The frequently played c5 break was much stronger here; again demonstrating the value of general moves in the Caro-Kann. } 20. g3 Bxe3 21. Nxe3 { My opponent makes a couple of sub-optimal moves, and again the c5 break is strong here, although black still stands better after my next move. } 21... Rfe8 22. Ng4 c5 { Finally, I made the obvious pawn break! } 23. Bxe4 Bxe4 24. Qd2 cxd4 25. cxd4 { Here I was confident that I was much better, and that I would have elementary and decisive play against white's weak pawns. Which is exactly what happened in the game, which can be replayed here: https://lichess.org/JXnxup8m }) 8... Nc6 9. O-O e6 10. Nf4 (10. Bf4 Bd6 { Bf4 is playable here too, but this just seems an open invitation to the bishop to come to this diagonal. } 11. Bxd6 Qxd6 12. Nd2 O-O { The b7-pawn is poisoned. Black has achieved a good position here. }) 10... Bg6 11. Nxg6 hxg6 12. Re1 Bd6 { Black has a small lead in development, and can again castle queenside in this line. White has several other positional plans available, but the positioning of the pieces shouldn't be too difficult for black in this line.
In fact, I relish facing this quiet and unambitious line, as I feel it's ideal in nature for experienced Caro-Kann players. Sorry Magnus, Fabi and Bobby! } *

