[UTCTime "10:52:38"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 { The Panov-Botvinnik Attack aesthetically resembles the Queen's Gambit, but has more scything intent. White plays directly, with the intention of creating a convoluted position immediately. White hopes for dynamic play, claiming that this will offer compensation for the often isolated d-pawn.
This is a challenging approach to handling the Caro-Kann, and one that I choose to implement as white. } 4... e6 { Unlike other lines, black has to lock in the light-squared bishop, as Qb3 is too strong if Bf5 is played here. } (4... Nf6 { Nf6 is also playable here, and I indeed diverged from mainline theory with this move against a 2100-rated opponent some months ago. The game continued as follows... } 5. Nc3 e6 6. c5 { c5 is a tempting endeavour in this position, but it's commonly tried in many of the d4 lines that I play as well, and it's not something that I particularly fear. Black can usually undermine this structure with b6 and a5, and is absolutely fine, or sometimes even better. } 6... Be7 { The immediate b6 is equally good, but I chose the quieter path. } 7. b4 a5 { This was a blunder. b6 was better here. } 8. b5 { White plays accurately with the best response. Now after b6, white has the foreboding c6, and black's game is rather terrible! } 8... Nbd7 9. a4 { White has gone wrong here, and black should counter-attack in the centre with e5. But I decided on a more dramatic (and unsound!) counterplay concept. } 9... Nxc5 { This isn't the best move, but Stockfish rates it as playable. I figured that white has done little other than move pawns, so if I removed a couple and gained a lead in development, then I shouldn't be much worse. } 10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. Ba3 Bxa3 12. Rxa3 d4 13. Nb1 e5 { Although black's central pawns appear ominous, white is still better here. } 14. Bc4 Bf5 15. Ne2 O-O 16. O-O Rc8 { Bringing my rook to the ubiquitous c-file. } 17. Ba2 Qe7 { Qd6 was better here, but attacking the rook is the right idea. } 18. Rf3 Be4 19. Rg3 Nh5 { Stockfish doesn't like this move, and prefers instead Bc2, attacking the queen. But I was quite happy with my position here. Indeed, white looks increasingly passive. } 20. Nd2 { A strange move from my opponent. White could instead have repositioned his rook elsewhere on the third rank. Black is better now. } 20... Nxg3 { Again Bc2 is better, but I wasn't about to decline the invitation of snaffling the rook. } 21. Nxg3 Bg6 22. f4 Bc2 23. Qg4 Kh8 24. Bd5 Bd3 { f5 is completely winning in this position, as black removes a pawn on a4 if white tries Qg5, or the central pawns become unstoppable after 25. Qd2 e4 } 25. Re1 f5 { Now after Qf3 black has equalised the game, because after e4, the queen on e7 is hanging. } 26. Qg5 Qxg5 27. fxg5 e4 { Unfortunately, my opponent has blundered here, and the position now becomes burdensome for white to play. } 28. Bxb7 Rc2 29. Nb3 Rc4 30. Ra1 Bc2 31. Nxa5 Rxa4 32. Rxa4 Bxa4 33. Bc6 d3 34. Nc4 e3 { Black's pawns are too strong now. } 35. Nxe3 d2 { forking the knights with f4 was better. } 36. Bf3 f4 37. Nd1 fxg3 { White can resign here, although my opponent struggled against the inevitable for a few more moves. You can replay this game here: https://lichess.org/kHFeGcki/ }) 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Nf3 { Both sides continue with regular knight deployment, but black has a decision to make here. The quiet Be7 is perfectly playable, but the Nimzo-Indianesque Bb4 can be considered more popular, and arguably more principled.
But we will examine both lines, and their particular idiosyncrasies. } 6... Bb4 (6... Be7 7. cxd5 exd5 { If Be7 here then isolating the d-pawn becomes marginally stronger. } 8. Bb5+ { White has other ideas as well, but this check is a natural developing move, as black can't safely exchange off bishops. 8...Bd7 9. Bxd7 Qxd7 10. Ne5 simply hands white a free tempo, while the knight establishes itself on the juicy e5 square. } { [%cal Gb8c6] } 8... Nc6 9. Ne5 { Castling kingside is white's other main move here, after which white will often put a knight on e5 anyway. } 9... Qb6 { This is the best way of holding the pawn. Black can also castle and sacrifice the c-pawn, hoping to gain counterplay from more rapid development, but this approach is inimical to the spirit of this repertoire. } 10. Be3 O-O 11. O-O Bf5 { Stockfish likes Bd6 here, but developing the light-squared bishop seems more natural. } 12. Rc1 Rac8 { The rook occupies the c-file, as is hugely common across all variations of the Caro-Kann. Black is okay here, but it seems fair to assert that white has a slight initiative, and is probably slightly better. } 13. g4 { This is why the Bb4 tends to be favoured, but, having said that, white's edge is nothing too serious, and players of this line must find the paradoxical looking g4 here in order to maintain an optimal advantage. } 13... Be6 { If this does occur, the bishop is considerably better on e6 than g6, otherwise h4 is threatened. } 14. Bxc6 bxc6 { A pawn storm should follow now from white on the kingside with f4. After anything else, black has complete equality. } 15. f4 Ne4 { Nxe4 is obviously playable here, but f5 seems more natural. } 16. f5 f6 { Black must kick out the e5-knight, after which white has several options. } 17. Nxe4 dxe4 { Black should be okay here, despite white's space advantage, and may even win a pawn with Bxa2. }) 7. cxd5 exd5 { Bg5 is also a pleasant idea here, but creating this slightly weird double-isolated d-pawn structure is best. } 8. Bd3 h6 { Preventing Bg5 is prudent here. Nc6 is also playable. } 9. O-O (9. h3 { There are several valid moves for white at this point. a3 puts the question to the bishop immediately, while developing the dark-squared bishop to f4 is also logical. This is also an interesting positional idea, preventing Bg4. } 9... O-O { There is no reason not to castle here, but Nc6 is also playable. } 10. O-O Nc6 11. Bf4 Bd6 { Challenging white's dark-squared bishop immediately. Be6 is also a logical continuation, and the e6 square is a natural place for the bishop if development to g4 is denied by white. } 12. Be5 (12. Bxd6 Qxd6 { Black could also exchange on d6 here, but this doesn't lead to anything particularly promising. }) 12... Bxe5 13. dxe5 Nd7 14. Bc2 Ndxe5 15. Nxe5 Nxe5 16. Qxd5 Qxd5 17. Nxd5 { AFter this massive simplification of the position, black comes up for air in a very stable state. }) 9... O-O 10. Bf4 Nc6 11. Rc1 Bg4 { When finally developing the light-squared bishop, it is natural for black to pin the f3 knight. Again, we have achieved complete equality here, and Stockfish even claims that black stands microscopically better. } *

