1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 { The Fantasy Variation is rarely seen at the highest level, being played in around 2% of games at the master level. But it does possess some logic - white attempts to build an ideal pawn centre with f3. This is visually seductive, but black can counter-attack effectively, while white's development is hampered by the f3 pawn. } 3... e6 { Stockfish likes g6 here, but e6 is played with thrice the regularity. } 4. Nc3 Bb4 { Nf6 is also playable, but this move is more enterprising. } 5. Ne2 { Several other moves also merit consideration, including Bd2. This variation is rarely played, and so theory remains relatively underdeveloped. } 5... Ne7 6. a3 (6. Qd2 { I have never faced this variation as black, so it's quite rare. In order to remedy the situation, I played a game against Stockfish level 6, and the computer chose Qd2 here. } 6... Nd7 7. h4 O-O 8. a3 Ba5 9. h5 h6 10. Rg1 { This is an unusual move, but bots are prone to such dalliances. Nonetheless, it is not entirely illogical, as white largely intends to castle queenside in this line, and the rook move can support a potential g-pawn assault. } 10... c5 { The c5 break seemed natural here, but the microprocessor instead prefers dxe4 or e5, both of which more favourably stymie white's centre. } 11. g4 cxd4 12. Qxd4 Nc6 13. Qf2 dxe4 14. f4 Bb6 { This was a mistake, as Be3 relieves a lot of the pressure on white's position. Despite this error, black has a comfortable game, and I was eventually able to defeat the computer in a fluctuating affair. You can see this far-from-perfect game here: https://lichess.org/LIPtC2l4 }) 6... Ba5 7. Bf4 O-O { Stockfish's choice here is Nd7, but Black can castle quite safely in this position, with white still relatively undeveloped. } 8. h4 { e5 may appear tempting, but after the thematic c5 break black is much better, and white's dark squares are tangibly weak. } (8. Qd2 Nd7 9. O-O-O { Another line worth considering here sees white castle kingside. Black would be advised to initiate active play rapidly here, in what could become a highly double-edged position. } 9... b5 10. h4 b4 11. axb4 Bxb4 { Black's attack has arrived first, and white will need to demonstrate some enterprising play in order to survive. }) 8... Nd7 9. h5 h6 10. Qd2 dxe4 (10... c5 { The classic c5 break also looks natural here, but this does invite the following scary riposte. } 11. Bxh6 gxh6 12. Qxh6 cxd4 13. Rh3 { This is why I would steer away from this line, and play the reassuringly quieter dxe4! } 13... dxc3 14. Rg3+ Ng6 { White will have to rely on a perpetual here, after Rxg6+, so in a sense this is an effective drawing line for black. But it needs to be remembered with perfect accuracy in order to be utilised, as black's king teeters on the brink of being mated. }) 11. fxe4 Nf6 { White's centre appears visually imposing, but in reality the two central pawns can become targets. Black is slightly ahead in development, while white has problems deploying some pieces and is potentially castling into strife on the queenside. But white has a space advantage and some hope of a kingside attack, so the position is largely equal and dynamically balanced. } 12. Qd3 Bc7 13. e5 Nfd5 *

