1. e4 c6 2. Nf3 { Of course, black is under no legal or ethical obligation to play 2. d4! And so the other customary second move for white is 2. Nf3. This approach is traditionally considerably less common than occupying the centre with a second pawn, but has been tried with much more regularity in the Internet era. } 2... d5 3. Nc3 { White has numerous possible third moves, but this is the most critical. White attempts to gain a small edge in development. } (3. d3 { This move has become voguish, not least because the engine Leela has chosen it in several games. However, I don't think it's anything to fear when facing a carbon-based opponent. } 3... dxe4 4. dxe4 Qxd1+ 5. Kxd1 { This line is recommended by Dutch GM Erwin l'Ami. And, to my untrained eye, it seems completely natural. Black can't hope to be better here, but white has effectively voluntarily sacrificed the advantage of being white immediately. } 5... Nf6 6. Nbd2 { 6. e5 is a mistake here, after 6...Nd5! } 6... Nbd7 7. b3 { White is going to be slightly worse here whatever is tried. Stockfish suggests 7. Ne1?!, but it's hard to imagine any human player ever choosing this. } 7... g6 { Black is just slightly better here, with a very solid structure, no weaknesses, a king that can still be castled, and decent development. } 8. Bb2 Bg7 { 8...Nc5 also merits consideration here. But this is a pragmatic and natural choice. } 9. Ne1 { Stockfish still wants this weird move. See the notes for other variations. } (9. Bd3 O-O 10. Ke2 Rd8 11. Rab1 Nc5 { Black is just better here. }) (9. Bc4 O-O 10. a4 Nb6 11. Bd3 Bg4 12. h3 Bxf3+ 13. Nxf3 Rad8 { This is another supremely comfortable variation for black. White has an impossible job to achieve any advantage or initiative whatsoever in this line. }) 9... O-O 10. Nd3 b6 11. g3 Bb7 12. Bg2 c5 { This is a position of complete equality. Other options fare no better for white. }) 3... dxe4 { Bg4 is also popular here. } 4. Nxe4 Nd7 { The book move here is typically Nf6. However, this doesn't appeal to me, as white can immediately ruin black's pawn structure. Instead, I prefer to support the knight on f6 via d7. It's a similar concept to the Karpov Variation in classical lines of the opening. } 5. d4 { It's important to also review and understand white's play after 5. Bc4, which is discussed below. } (5. Bc4 { There is some debate over the best move in this position. Stockfish deems central control with 5. d4 to be superior, but 5. Bc4 is also played with regularity, and does poses black some specific and tricky problems. } 5... Ngf6 6. Neg5 { Taking the knight on f6 here is completely harmless, whereas this perhaps synthetic move is much more challenging. } 6... e6 { White must prevent the threat of Bxf7#. } 7. Qe2 { Ganging up on the e-pawn. Black must be very careful here, as h6 is disastrous, allowing Nxf7 and then Qxe6, with an irresistible attack. } (7. Nxf7 { This knight sacrifice is unsound, but often attempted online, and definitely worth reviewing. } 7... Kxf7 { Black can safely take the knight. } 8. Ng5+ Ke8 { Black has two decent squares here, but Ke8 seems more natural, as it opens up the diagonal for the dark-squared bishop, as opposed to blocking it. } 9. Nxe6 { This looks quite scary, but the main thing here is not to hang your queen! Trust me, I've done it before! } 9... Qa5 { This is the ideal square for the queen, as now Qe2 can be met by Ne5, which is protected by the queen, thus neutering the potential discovered attack. } 10. O-O Ne5 11. Bb3 Bxe6 12. Bxe6 Bd6 13. Re1 Kd8 { Black's king is threatening to escape now... } 14. d4 Ng6 15. Bd2 Qh5 16. Qxh5 Nxh5 { After the queen exchange, black should be better. }) 7... Nd5 { Qe7 is also playable here, but it looks rather ugly. } 8. d4 (8. Nxf7 Kxf7 { Black has no choice after this unsound sacrifice. } 9. d4 Be7 10. O-O Rf8 11. Bd3 Kg8 { Black has castled manually, and white's speculative attack has rapidly fizzled out. }) 8... h6 { Now h6 forces the knight back. } 9. Ne4 Qc7 10. O-O Be7 { White is much better after Bd6, Nxd6, Qxd6, after which our opponent has myriad options. } 11. Ng3 O-O { White has several possible plans here, and is possibly slightly better. Re1 and c3 are likely human moves, but Stockfish sees the potential of the following approach. } 12. Nh5 (12. Ne5 { The immediate Ne5 is also playable here. Black can't exchange, as the resulting e5-pawn becomes a thorn in the black camp. } 12... c5 { But the c5 break is strong and logical here. }) 12... N5f6 13. Bf4 Bd6 { Bxd6, Ne5, and Nxf6 are all equally playable here. The number one computer line is as follows... } 14. Bxd6 Qxd6 15. Nxf6+ Nxf6 { Black is slightly behind in development, but should be fine. }) (5. Qe2 { Online chancers will sometimes have an opportunistic stab with Qe2. This is a sub-par move, but it's important to be aware of the following trap. } 5... Ngf6 { Black must now avoid the natural looking Ngf6, as... } 6. Nd6# { This ends the game abruptly! Instead, 5...Ndf6, 5...Qc7, or 5...e6 eliminate this threat. }) 5... Ngf6 { White could also develop further here, possibly with Qe2. But this capture is the most logical continuation. } 6. Nxf6+ { Stockfish likes exf6 here, but surely more natural for a human player is to recapture with the knight. } (6. Bd3 { Buttressing the centre in this fashion is also a popular idea in the Two Knights Caro-Kann, and this was played against me by a 2180-rated opponent in a recent game. } 6... e6 { It was better here to exchange the knights and then win a tempo on the bishop with Nf6. } 7. O-O Nxe4 8. Bxe4 Nf6 { I managed to gain a tempo here anyway, but already this idea is less effective; underlying the importance of timing. } 9. Bd3 Bd6 10. Re1 O-O 11. h3 { This was completely unnecessary, as black cannot pin the knight due to my early e6, nor can I do anything useful on the g4 square with my f6 knight. This is simply a waste of time, and enables black to equalise. } 11... b6 { This weakening push wasn't necessarily the best approach; arguably c5 is better. However, black stands pretty much equal. } 12. Bg5 Be7 { Not really necessary. I could have continued with my plan with Bb7, as the knight pin achieves little of note. } 13. c3 Bb7 { White has a better centre, but black is absolutely solid. } 14. Bc2 Qc7 15. Ne5 Rac8 16. Qd3 g6 { After some hesitant and insipid play from black, white has achieved an unsubtle yet dangerous attack. } 17. Bh6 Rfe8 18. Re3 { This rook lift is a mistake, as black can now intrude on white's centre with c5. } 18... Bf8 { But I was concerned about my dark squares, and missed this elementary idea. } 19. Bg5 Be7 20. Rg3 c5 { Finally! I manage to get in the c5 break, open up my b7 bishop, and have achieved completely equality at the very least here. } 21. Bh6 { Black is better now, and I managed to win the game from this position, despite one humungous blunder! See here: https://lichess.org/32dW77wX } 21... Be4 { This game demonstrates some of the important ideas and positional considerations in this line, many of which (c5 break, strong c6 and e6 pawns, bishop coming alive on b7) should be familiar by now, and will become increasingly engrained as later sections unfold. }) 6... Nxf6 7. c3 { White has numerous options here, but this sturdy pawn move, in the spirit of the Exchange Variation, is as worthy as any. } 7... Bg4 { Bf5 is also perfectly fine, but this knight pin seems more principled. } 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 { White can retreat the bishop here, but this can be troublesome after 8...Bh5 9. g4 Bg6...as the piece can become vulnerable to g-pawn and h-pawn advances. Relinquishing the bishop pair leads to a more practically playable position, even if it is not to be relished in principle. } 9... e6 10. Be2 Bd6 11. O-O O-O { White has a slight edge here, due to the retention of the bishop pair, and a somewhat stronger centre. But black's position is not difficult to play, all of its pieces are well-placed, and we can now play for some of the established motifs in the Caro-Kann. } 12. Re1 Qc7 { Stockfish prefers a5 here, but this is a natural developing move. If 12. Be3 then Nd5 gives black a very decent position. } 13. a3 Rad8 14. Bg5 Be7 { This disruptive bishop thrust delays black's c5 break. } 15. Bd3 c5 { But it's coming imminently anyway! } 16. dxc5 Qxc5 { White is still marginally better here, and perhaps something could be made of the queenside pawn majority. But this is an eminently playable position for black. } *

