1. e4 c6 2. Bc4 { Caro-Kann players should rejoice when they see 2. Bc4 on the board! The Hillbilly Attack is a desultory novelty, which simply allows black to gain an immediate initiative. It might catch an unsuspecting opponent by surprise, but once you have learned the lines it should pose no difficulty whatsoever. } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 { All white has achieved here is to misplace the light-squared bishop. } 4. Bb5+ { Stockfish prefers Bb3 here, but from my experience this move is the more common rejoinder. } (4. Bb3 { The Hillbilly Attack is very rarely seen at anything above 2000 level, as it's known to be a pretty lousy opening. So I've trawled through the archives to find this miniature from a few years ago. My opponent did indeed follow Stockfish's recommendation here. But it didn't do him or her much good! } 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 { This prophylactic move isn't really necessary, but white has so fundamentally abandoned sound opening play that black has time for such cautious play, while still being better. } 6. d4 Bg4 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. h3 Bh5 { Much better here is simply to take the knight, and either split the kingside pawns after gxf3, or win the d-pawn after Qxf3. But I wanted to keep the bishop on the board, and figured my low-rated opponent would weaken the kingside, which could prove fatal. } 9. g4 Bg6 10. Be3 { Black is much better now. g5 was the only way to fight for equality. } 10... e6 11. O-O { Castling into trouble! } 11... Bd6 12. Bg5 { This is far too ambitious, and is unjustified by black's precarious position. Black must attempt to secure the kingside with Ne2. } 12... h5 13. Ne2 { This is too late now, as the kingside will already inevitably be peeled open like an orange. } 13... hxg4 14. hxg4 Qc7 { Be4 was even stronger here, forcing the e2-knight away from the kingside in order to allow the Queen to defend the knight on f3. } 15. Ng3 { This is far too late, and clearly doesn't work. } 15... Bxg3 { White can resign here. } 16. fxg3 Qxg3# { But instead of resigning, black allowed mate! You can replay this game here: https://lichess.org/XBZxHmJe. Both my opponent and I have improved a lot since then. }) 4... Bd7 { Again, Stockfish prefers Nc6 here, but... } 5. Bxd7+ Nxd7 { I have found that most opponents will exchange here, at which point black has been handed a lead in development on a silver platter. Equally, retreating the bishop to e2 represents pretty feeble play. } 6. Qe2 e6 7. Nf3 Ngf6 { Black is just better with any sensible move. } 8. d4 Bd6 9. O-O O-O { White is solid enough, but black has a lead in development, and can now continue with typical ideas in the Caro-Kann without being impeded at all. Black can now build up c-file pressure, and white must play accurately and resourcefully in order to retain anything approaching equality. } *

