1. e4 c6 2. c4 { 2. c4 is the third most popular second move for white when faced with the Caro-Kann, having been proffered just over 1,000 times in the Chess365 masters database (compared to over 17,000 for d4). This opening is sometimes referred to as the Accelerated Panov Attack. } 2... d5 3. exd5 (3. e5 { In a game that I played some months ago, my 2192-rated opponent deviated from the main lines immediately, with this pseudo-Advanced Variation attempt. } 3... Bf5 { Having never faced this move order, it seemed natural to place the bishop outside of my yet to be formed pawn triangle. But Stockfish notes that d4 is much better here. } 4. Nc3 e6 5. f4 h6 6. Nf3 Ne7 7. c5 Nd7 8. d4 { Black could have achieved a big advantage at any time with d4, but this move never occurred to me, and I instead chose more stereotypical development.
Eventually, black played d4 and is okay here, although white is probably slightly better. } 8... b6 9. b4 a5 10. Ba3 { This moves makes little sense, as it just allows black to fatally undermine white's pawn structure. White never really recovered from this mistake in the game. } 10... axb4 11. Bxb4 bxc5 12. dxc5 Bg4 13. Be2 Nf5 { Simply by employing straightforward, natural moves black is much better. } 14. O-O Bxc5+ { Nd3 winning the exchange was superior. } 15. Bxc5 Nxc5 16. h3 Bxh3 { Here I took the liberty of sacrificing my bishop. } 17. gxh3 d4 18. Nb1 O-O { d3 was completely winning, but observe the quality of black's pieces in comparison to the white army. } 19. Bd3 Ne3 { Finally, black wins the exchange. I must admit that I missed a hanging queen on the very next move! But black won handily, apart from this major oversight! You can replay this game here: https://lichess.org/Ekv4mXgh/black#38 }) 3... cxd5 4. cxd5 { White can also transpose to the Panov-Botvinnik Attack here with d4. } (4. d3 { A 2140-rated opponent also tried this curious move against me some months ago. The game didn't go well for white... https://lichess.org/KckYcwQD }) 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 Nxd5 6. Nf3 (6. Nxd5 { It is not uncommon for white to take here, even though this hands black a clear advantage. This was a line chosen by my 2176-rated opponent in a game played a few months ago. } 6... Qxd5 { Black should be enamoured with this position. The queen can't be kicked out by a knight, the opposing d-pawn is isolated, and white has gained no developmental advantage whatsoever in exchange for ruining its structure. You should rapidly snatch the hand of any opponent offering this line! } 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. d4 Bf5 9. h3 { White's play has been quite anaemic, and the computer already wants an aggressive pawn thrust here with e5. However, I chose a quieter response, after which black is still comfortably better. } 9... e6 10. Bd3 Bxd3 11. Qxd3 Bb4+ 12. Bd2 Bxd2+ 13. Qxd2 O-O { I was perfectly content to castle here, delighted with the serene scene on the board. But Rd8, immediately ganging up on the isolated d-pawn, was stronger. } 14. O-O Rfd8 15. Rfd1 h6 16. a3 Na5 { Eyeing the b3 square. } 17. Qf4 Nb3 18. Rab1 Rac8 { Black has the c-file presence that is so coveted by Caro-Kann players. } 19. Qe3 Rc2 20. Qe5 Qxe5 21. dxe5 Rxd1+ 22. Rxd1 Rxb2 { This seemed a hugely tempting continuation; black wins a pawn. But white has counterplay now for the first time in the game. } 23. Rd8+ Kh7 24. Rd7 Na5 25. Rxf7 Nc6 26. h4 a5 27. h5 Rb3 28. Rc7 Rxa3 29. Rxb7 a4 { The a-pawn appears dangerous, but it is an illusory threat. White should be able to encircle the pawn with rook, knight and king, while black's e6 pawn is also particularly weak and vulnerable. The game should definitely be a draw with accurate play. } 30. Rb6 Rc3 31. Ra6 a3 32. Nd2 Nb4 { White allowed black to gain some important tempi, and I went on to win. You can replay the whole game here: https://lichess.org/Pg9IfoqM }) 6... a6 { This is a judicious move, preventing the knight and bishop from coming to b5. Black must be happy with this position after six moves, considering the isolated white d-pawn. } 7. d4 Nc6 8. Bd3 Bg4 { This is another Caro-Kann line where pinning the bishop is preferable. } 9. O-O e6 10. Be4 { This is the best try for white here, as any other move leads to complete equality. } 10... Be7 { Ignoring Bxd5 is best here. Nf6 is certainly playable, but white can take on c6 and tarnish black's pawn structure. If Nxd5 exd5 here, black is just slightly better, with an extremely comfortable position. } 11. Qb3 { This seems as if it may be a challenging line, but this impression is illusory. } (11. Nxd5 exd5 { If Nxd5 exd5 here, black is slightly better, and has enjoyed a pleasantly untroubled opening. }) 11... O-O 12. Nxd5 { Black can simply castle, before a possible exchange takes place on d5. White cannot snaffle the b-pawn, see the alternative variation below... } (12. Qxb7 Na5 { The b-pawn is emphatically not hanging, as Na5 traps the queen! }) 12... exd5 13. Qxd5 Bxf3 { Black has several forcing lines here, each of which leads to the winning of the d-pawn. } 14. gxf3 Qxd5 15. Bxd5 Nxd4 16. Rd1 (16. Bxb7 Rab8 17. Bxa6 Nc2 18. Rb1 Na3 19. Ra1 Nc2 { Taking on b7 just leads to a dead draw by repetition. }) 16... Bf6 { This is now a very drawish position, but it wouldn't be too bold to conclude that black's primary mission of achieving equality has been accomplished. } *

