1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 { 2. Nc3 is another rarely played, but acceptable move. } 2... d5 { d4 here just transposes straight into the mainline Caro-Kann. Nf3 transposes into the Two Knights Variation. Other moves are also playable, but here we will examine the following variation. } 3. exd5 cxd5 4. d4 { Black has nothing to fear here, blocking in the c-pawn is not in white's favour. } 4... Nf6 5. Bf4 a6 { Again, it's prudent to prevent the knight, in particular, from coming to b5, particularly once the bishop has been situated on the h2-b8 diagonal. } 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Ne5 { As is so often the case, the e5 square is a valuable outpost for white. Learning to deal with this is a critical skill in the Caro-Kann. } 7... e6 { The computer favours Bd7 here, but this is fine as well. } 8. Nxc6 bxc6 { This exchange practically invites an imminent c5 break. } 9. Be2 { Na4 is an interesting attempt here to reinforce c5, but it doesn't achieve a great deal, and black can continue to develop without impediment. } 9... Bd6 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. O-O O-O { Black has an excellent position. The light-squared bishop can later be developed to b7, or sometimes a6. } *

