[StudyName "Complete Black c6 Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Inviting the Slav – 3. Nf3"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iFA7tZcn/0lPo8wdT"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/wub1234"]
[UTCDate "2021.01.05"]
[UTCTime "10:54:14"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. d4 c6 2. c4 d5 3. Nf3 { The bedrock of this repertoire will be the Slav and Semi-Slav positions in the Queen's Gambit. Now I must confess that I'm not a profound expert on the precise classification of certain Queen's Gambit lines, but you don't need to be! All you need to know it what to play, and what not to play! } 3... Nf6 4. Nc3 (4. e3 e6 { This leads to positions covered in Chapter 21. }) (4. cxd5 cxd5 { White can exchange pawns here as well. The challenge for black here is developing the light-squared bishop effectively, while also ensuring that black doesn't garner an advantage from either the light squares or a potentially unprotected b-pawn } 5. Qb3 { In many lines, the White queen can come to b3, and this can be problematical if the c8 bishop is not protecting b7. Equally, playing b6 to defend can fatally weaken the squares on the queenside, so black must tread carefully in many of these positions. } 5... e6 { Black has many possible moves here, but accepting that the light-squared bishop will be locked in, developing quickly and castling is as sound as any other plan. Also, playable are Nc6 and Ne4. } 6. Bf4 Nc6 7. e3 { This line requires black to play actively. Bd6 is an okay move, but won't achieve true equality. } 7... Ne4 8. h3 (8. h4 Na5 9. Bb5+ Bd7 10. Qa4 Nc6 11. Nc3 a6 12. Be2 { Black has an excellent position now. } 12... Rc8 { Occupying the c-file is almost always worthwhile in this repertoire. } 13. O-O Bb4 14. Qb3 Bxc3 15. bxc3 Na5 16. Qb4 Nxc3 { Black has won a pawn here. } 17. Bd3 Bb5 18. Rfc1 Bxd3 19. Rxc3 Rxc3 20. Qxc3 Bc4 21. Qb4 f6 { Black must castle 'manually' here. } 22. Nd2 Bb5 23. a4 Bc6 24. Rc1 Kf7 { Finally, the king escapes to safety. Black will have decent winning chances in the endgame. })) (4. b3 Bf5 { If white plays b3 at any time, black has the freedom to liberate the light-squared bishop, as Qb3 is no longer possible. In this position, developing quickly and castling at the first opportunity is to be recommended. } 5. e3 e6 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Qxd3 Nbd7 8. O-O Bd6 9. Nbd2 O-O { This isn't necessarily the sharpest line, nor perfect play from black, but this offers an indurated foundation on which to build. } 10. e4 dxe4 11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. Qxe4 Nf6 { This idea of exchanging on e4 and then winning a tempo on the queen is also a standard concept in this opening. White has a slight space advantage here, but nothing of which black should be unduly apprehensive. }) 4... e6 { I aim for this triangle of pawns on c6, d5, and e6, which should be instantly familiar by now. However, do check out the sidelines, as white need not allow this. } 5. e3 (5. cxd5 exd5 { Again, white can exchange here, and opening up the light-squared bishop makes positional sense for black. } 6. Qb3 { White now has the option of immediately popping the question to the light-squared bishop regarding his future whereabouts. I would recommend playing quietly here, and simply castling, as this will make untangling the queenside easier. } 6... Be7 7. Bg5 { Bg5 is often played here, and in these lines generally. Stockfish often favours h6 in these positions, but black has several adequate ideas, including Qb6 and castling. } 7... Qb6 (7... h6 8. Bxf6 Bxf6 { h6 essentially forces white to forego the bishop pair; always a boon! }) (7... O-O { Black can also just simply castle here, and has achieved complete equality. }) 8. Qxb6 axb6 { Qb6 may seem a trifle ugly here, but opening the a-file is in black's favour. }) (5. Bg5 { This is another popular and aggressive try. We must tread carefully here, as white can develop a formidable attack if black plays sloppily. } 5... Be7 { Stockfish likes dxc4 here, but this move is more in keeping with our approach elsewhere. Other ideas, such as h6 and Nbd7, are also playable, but the text move is rock solid. } 6. e3 Nbd7 7. Bd3 dxc4 8. Bxc4 b5 9. Bd3 a6 { Now we have found another way of weaponising the Slav system. However, e4 is potentially dangerous in this line, so precise play is demanded. } 10. O-O { e4 is premature and impatient here, as after 10...c5 11. e5 Nd5, black has a fine position. } 10... Bb7 11. Qe2 O-O { The c5 break and Rc8 also merit consideration. Again, black should be plodding on familiar ground here. }) 5... Nbd7 6. Bd3 (6. Qc2 { Also possible in this position is to menacingly align the queen and bishop, and point them at the h7 pawn. } 6... Bd6 { Straightforward development is best here. } (6... Be7 7. c5 { Sometimes black can be tempted to advance the c-pawn to c5. If black has developed correctly then this shouldn't happen later in the game, meaning that the white queenside is still undeveloped, and making the following tactic possible. } 7... b6 { Black simply looks to immediately undermine white's pawn chain. } 8. b4 a5 { And continues to do so! a3 obviously doesn't work here, as the rook is hanging. } 9. cxb6 (9. b5 Bb7 { b5 is white's best hope here, but black definitely has a slight edge. It's important to absorb this concept of the b-pawn and a-pawn counter- attack against the pawn chain, as you will almost certainly encounter an opponent taking a punt with c5 at some point. }) 9... axb4 { cxb6 is a big mistake here, due to this capture. })) 6... dxc4 { This capture on c4 is better when black's bishop has already been developed. Otherwise, black has surrendered a tempo for no reason. } 7. Bxc4 b5 8. Bd3 (8. Be2 { Also possible here is the less ambitious, but more solid, Be2. Black should now continue developing. } 8... Bb7 9. a3 a6 10. b4 { White is slightly worse after any other move. } 10... a5 11. Rb1 axb4 12. axb4 Nd5 13. Nxd5 exd5 14. O-O Bd6 15. Qc2 O-O { An unusual line, with black's light-squared bishop looking quite feeble. But still one that enables black to equalise. }) 8... a6 { I first saw Kramnik play this system around 20 years ago. Hopefully, white will castle here, after which we can sedately complete our development with Bb7 (although Stockfish is addicted to pawn breaks, and likes c5).
But black might not play ball, and could instead rudely disturb our plans. } 9. e4 { This isn't the best move for white, but merits attention as we cannot simply routinely blitz out Bb7 now, as e5 would be way too strong. } (9. O-O Bb7 { If black castles here, Stockfish likes the immediate c5 break. However, Bb7 is just as good, and completes black's system of development on the queenside. } 10. e4 c5 { This is a very principled and typical thrust and counter-thrust in Slav / Queen's Gambit positions. As in the mainline discussed here, white now has a choice between d5 and e5. } 11. Be3 cxd4 12. Bxd4 Bc5 13. Bc2 O-O { However, if white chooses to develop more quietly then black can achieve all of its opening aims, with an excellent position. }) 9... c5 { Instead, we must play the c5 break immediately. Bb7 is too slow, and allows white a massive positional advantage after e5. } 10. d5 { White can also try e5 here, but this move is more disruptive. } (10. e5 { But e5 is sharp as well. } 10... cxd4 { The only move. } 11. Ne2 (11. Nxb5 { Black players might hope for Ne2, but this is arguably the most principled response. Equalising for black after this semi-sacrifice requires particularly precise play. } 11... axb5 12. exf6 gxf6 13. O-O Qb6 14. Qe2 Ba6 15. Rd1 { Black has an extra pawn, but white's positional compensation should be blindingly obvious. Our king has no obvious safe haven, and black really needs to exchange off some pieces rapidly. } 15... Rd8 16. a4 { However, this is the only move to fight for an advantage. After anything else, our minor pieces will begin to find the right squares, and black will steadily equalise. } 16... Nc5 17. axb5 Bb7 { As always, the bishop is well-placed here, particularly with such a yawning diagonal at its disposal. } 18. Ra3 Be7 { Black is beginning to equalise. Snapping off the d3-knight is also logical here. } 19. Bc4 Rg8 { Black's king is now quite safe, our rooks are on decent files, the c5-knight and b7-bishop are powerful; black has completely equalised.
What I will say is that this is one of the most challenging variations anywhere. } 20. Bd2 { Stockfish assesses this position as completely equal.
What I will say is that this is one of the most challenging variations anywhere in this repertoire. I don't think that I've ever faced it, but it's certainly a quagmire of variations, and one well worth examining diligently. }) 11... Nd5 12. Nexd4 Bb7 13. O-O Be7 14. Bd2 Rc8 { Occupying the c-file is hardly ever a bad idea! } 15. Qe2 O-O { Black has a very promising position here. }) 10... Bb7 { exd5 is not really playable here. Either e5 or Nxd5 hand white a niggling advantage. } 11. O-O Qc7 { c4 is also possible here. } 12. dxe6 fxe6 { Obviously we would prefer not to shatter our pawn structure in this fashion, but we can't get everything we want while playing black! } 13. Ng5 Qc6 { We can defend the pawn without too much difficulty here, and Stockfish actually assesses this position as pretty much equal. Black's queenside play and light square domination offer satisfactory compensation for the e-pawn. } 14. Be2 { This is the only way for white to maintain even a smidgen of an edge. } 14... b4 { Although b4 or c4 is coming here, and black has plenty of play. } (14... c4 { c4 is fine here, but does allow white some play on the kingside. } 15. Bg4 b4 16. Ne2 e5 { This should be okay for black, despite its precarious appearance. }) 15. Na4 { The knight is now confined to this undesirable square. Going back to b1 is worse. } 15... h6 16. Bh5+ Ke7 17. Nf7 Nxh5 18. Nxh8 Qxe4 { This is highly tactical, but shows the possibilities that can appear on the board in this opening. All the time, black has great compensation for any positional issues due to its extremely powerful light-squared bishop and queen tandem. Indeed, white must play f3 here in order to survive. } 19. f3 { The dust settles with black slightly better, although there is quite clearly much more play in this position! } *

