1. d4 c6 2. c4 d5 3. cxd5 cxd5 { White can also opt for the simplifying cxd5. Stockfish considers this to be a second-rate move, but it can pose mere human players some problems!
It should be noted as well that this has already transposed into the Caro-Kann Exchange Variation, but we're going to examine a different line and approach from black here. } 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Nc3 { Qb3 is possible here, but shouldn't be feared; neither the b-pawn nor d-pawn are hanging. } (5. Qb3 { A fledgling Qb3 can be tried here as well, although it doesn't promise anything extraordinary for white. } 5... Nc6 6. Bf4 e6 { The light-squared bishop must be closed in here, due to the b-pawn threat from the queen. The knight can also be challenged immediately with Nh5, but I prefer this solid developing move. } 7. e3 Nh5 { Now we should challenge the bishop. } 8. Be5 f6 9. Bg3 Nxg3 10. hxg3 Bd6 11. Nc3 { Black has a flexible and sound position, and now has a pleasant choice of moves. } 11... Bd7 { In this position, 12. Qxb7 Rb8 13. Qa6 Rxb2 looks extremely bad for white. } 12. Bd3 f5 { Cutting off the light-squared bishop is essential, due to the gaping h-file. } 13. Rc1 Rc8 14. Nb5 Be7 15. O-O a6 16. Nc3 O-O { Black has once again achieved complete equality here. }) 5... Nc6 { However, black does need to be deploy the light-squared bishop carefully. In this position, Bf5 allows Qb3, which is suddenly far more awkward.
a6 is also playable here, while e6 is too committal, closing in the aforementioned bishop. White instead benefits from keeping options open with this piece. } 6. Bf4 a6 { Black can also bring the light-squared bishop out here, but, although sound, that can lead to some highly tactical positions, in which black is teetering on the brink of disaster. This precautionary sortie can be considered more sensible. } 7. Qb3 e6 { We must tolerate shutting in the c8-bishop here. Black should develop quickly in order to equalise. } 8. Rc1 Bd6 { Be7 seems perfectly playable as well. } 9. Bxd6 Qxd6 10. e3 O-O { Black's light-squared bishop is problematical, but our position is, essentially, largely solid. } 11. Bd3 b5 { Black's knight can now often head for the buttressed c-4 square, particularly if black makes no provisions to prevent this plan. } 12. O-O Bd7 13. Ne2 Rac8 { Occupying the c-file once more... } 14. h3 Na5 { Black has largely equalised here, and Stockfish believes that black is better after any move other than Qd1, although this isn't terrible difficult to find. } 15. Qd1 Nc4 { I have placed this knight on this square so, so, so many times in similar positions! White does have a minor problem here, as this is now a pesky knight, b3 cannot be played, as the knight will jump to b2, and black has some nagging c-file pressure. Black's only sub-par piece is the light-squared bishop. } *

