1. d4 c6 2. c4 d5 3. e3 { White can also initiate the classic Queen's Gambit formation with the early 3. e3. } 3... Nf6 { e6 is also fine here. } 4. Nf3 e6 5. b3 { b3 is another idea in these lines, which diverts away from the typical Slav setups discussed in chapter 19. b3 is also often played after Qc2 here. } 5... Bd6 6. Bb2 (6. c5 Bc7 { If c5 here, black can put the bishop on c7, and begin undermining the pawn chain with b6, a5, etc. }) 6... O-O { White has very little as of yet, but is playing for long-term dynamism. } 7. Bd3 Nbd7 { It isn't advisable to capture the c4-pawn in this variation, as white has an enviable centre after bxc4. } 8. Qc2 b6 { Ever the aggressor, Stockfish prefers c5 here, craving the immediate pawn break. However, this patient developing move is more in keeping with the ethos of this repertoire. Qe7 and Qc7 are also playable. } 9. cxd5 cxd5 { Black should exchange in the centre here, and claim that the light-squared bishop is weak. } 10. Qc6 { This is the only way that white can fight for an advantage. After 0-0, Qe7 and Bb7 result in total equality. } 10... Qc7 11. Qxc7 Bxc7 12. O-O { Stockfish lists Ba3 as promising here, but castling is a much more likely human response. } 12... Bb7 { White has no more than a negligible edge now. } 13. Rc1 { Logical play, but black has several decent responses. } 13... Bd6 { Immediately occupying the c-file with Rfc8 is fine as well. } 14. Ne5 Rfc8 { Middle game skills will now decide the outcome of this game. Both sides must cope with bishops that are nibbling on granite, and the pawn structure is completely symmetrical. Black cannot have any complaints about the opening outcome. } *

[Event "Complete Black c6 Repertoire: System Openings - The London System – 2. Bf4 and The Stonewall Attack"]
[Date "2021.01.05"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
