[StudyName "Complete Black c6 Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "System Openings - The London System – 2. Bf4 and The Stonewall Attack"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iFA7tZcn/5fHWwOMg"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/wub1234"]
[UTCDate "2021.01.05"]
[UTCTime "10:54:38"]
[Orientation "black"]

{ The London System is particularly voguish, not least because it has been employed by one Sven Magnus Øen Carlsen (Norway). Personally, I think sacrificing the advantage of the white pieces by playing 'system' openings is weedy, but what do I know?! }
1. d4 c6 2. Bf4 { White is setting up the London System, but black shouldn't be unduly concerned. } (2. e3 { Another somewhat less popular system opening is The Stonewall Attack. We definitely shouldn't fear this, as it's essentially a very poor setup for white! } 2... d5 3. Bd3 { White plays Bd3, as in the London System, but is about to commit pawn masochism! } 3... Nd7 4. f4 { White also intends to play c3, but if c3 here then e5! gives black a significant edge. } 4... Ngf6 { Black has many possible moves here. c5 and g6 are both playable, but there is nothing wrong with this simple development. } 5. c3 { c4 is much better here, but this zig-zag pawn structure is the foundation of the Stonewall. It is not to be encouraged, as it criminally weakens the light squares in the hope that white can attack on the kingside. With correct play, this is a forlorn hope. } 5... Ne4 { Probably the most principled move here is to occupy the light squares immediately. } 6. Nd2 Ndf6 7. Nxe4 Nxe4 8. Bxe4 dxe4 9. b3 Bf5 { Black has the bishop pair and is slightly better here. White has made a lot of committal pawn moves and does not have an ideal structure. } 10. Ba3 { However, this move in a nice try to gain some advantage from the opening. } 10... h5 11. Ne2 h4 12. h3 e6 13. Bxf8 Kxf8 { White can't castle either side, while Black also can't castle, but can potentially shift the rook to g6 via h6, effectively castling the king manually and building a dangerous attack. g5 is also coming. I would much rather play black here, although the computer deems this position equal. }) 2... Nf6 (2... d5 { In common with the rest of this repertoire, black can also play 2...d5 comfortably. This is usually my choice, even though it's not the computer's first instinct. } 3. Nf3 { 3. Nd2 is possible here, but this move is more likely. } 3... Nf6 { Bf5 is also playable, but this would be my preference, ensuring that the light-squared bishop can continue to protect the pawn on b7. } 4. e3 e6 { Again, Bf5 is playable here. } 5. c3 Nbd7 6. Nbd2 Be7 7. Bd3 { There are a few possible moves here, but we will examine two lines in particular. } 7... Nh5 { Trying to remove the London player's beloved dark-squared bishop is a strong concept. } (7... O-O 8. Ne5 Nxe5 9. Bxe5 c5 10. O-O b6 { 10...c4 is also a strong move here, but I would prefer to keep the tension. } 11. Qe2 Bb7 12. Rad1 c4 13. Bc2 b5 { White has achieved no advantage from the opening here whatsoever. }) 8. O-O O-O 9. Qe2 Nxf4 10. exf4 c5 { This position is fine for black, but the doubled pawn on f4 can actually be quite pesky. I therefore prefer the other line analysed here with no Nh5. } 11. dxc5 Nxc5 12. Bc2 Bd6 { Black has easily achieved equality here. }) 3. e3 { e3 isn't necessarily the best move, but is to be expected from London players. } (3. c3 d5 4. Nf3 Bf5 5. e3 e6 6. Bd3 Bd6 7. O-O Bxd3 8. Qxd3 Bxf4 9. exf4 Nbd7 10. Ne5 O-O { If white instead attempts an early c3, black can play a much more customary system, and equalise with some ease. }) 3... Qb6 { Forcing an early concession from white. } 4. b3 d5 { Now we finally uncork our customary second move. } 5. Nf3 (5. Bd3 { This is another likely move here, as it fits neatly into typical London plans. } 5... Bg4 { This is a strong move in this position, as the London is a system opening, and our opponent is likely to follow the usual developmental scheme. } 6. Nf3 { Qd2 or Ne2 are marginally better here for white, but they go against the grain of usual London System development. } 6... e6 7. O-O c5 { Now we can play the c5 break safely. } 8. Nbd2 Bd6 9. Bb5+ Nbd7 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. h3 Bh5 12. dxc5 Qxc5 { Black has largely equalised here, and Bxd7 or c4 are the only sensible options in this position. } 13. c4 { And this is probably more uncompromising. } 13... O-O { But black can just castle into equality! }) 5... Nh5 { Attempting to eliminate the pride of white's position makes sense. } 6. Be5 { If white goes back to g3 then black simply extracts the bishop with Nxg3. } 6... Nd7 7. Bd3 Nxe5 8. Nxe5 g6 { Black has to be flexible in this system, and g6 is more logical than e6 here. } 9. O-O Bg7 10. Nd2 O-O { Black has achieved complete equality here, while also removing the pesky 'London' dark-squared bishop. } *

