1. d4 c6 2. g3 { Initialising a g2 fianchetto of the bishop on the second move is an interesting idea. But 1...c6 is really an ideal response to this concept, helping to concretise the h1-a8 diagonal. } (2. c3 Nf6 3. Bf4 { To briefly mention, 2. c3 can rapidly transpose into London System territory. } (3. Nf3 d5 4. Bf4 Bf5 5. Nbd2 e6 6. e3 h6 7. Qb3 Qb6 8. h3 Nbd7 9. Be2 Be7 10. O-O O-O { If black attempts to diverge from London System setups, black can equalise in elementary fashion. }) 3... d5 4. e3 Qb6) (2. Bg5 { This move, reminiscent of the Trompowsky Attack, is also possible here. In nearly 600,000 games in the Chess365 masters database, this position has been reached precisely once! Drozdov (2449) and Maki Uuro (2425) played this opening in 2003. So this is an almost entirely unploughed field! } 2... d5 { It's possible to put the question to the bishop immediately with 2...h6, or attack the unguarded b-pawn with 2...Qb6. But let's opt for our usual approach, which is perfectly fine here. } 3. Nf3 h6 (3... Nd7 { This move, akin to the Karpov Variation in the Caro-Kann, is also perfectly fine, enabling us to play Ngf6 next. } 4. Nbd2 Ngf6 5. e3 e6 6. Bd3 Be7 7. O-O O-O { h6, c5, and b6 all also lead to equality. }) (3... Nf6 { Another option here is to play 3...Nf6. If white takes the knight, we can be comfortable with our doubled pawns given that our opponent has surrendered the bishop and also that this opening scheme makes little sense! } 4. e3 Qb6 5. Nbd2 Bf5 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. cxd3 Nbd7 8. O-O e6 9. Rb1 h6 10. Bxf6 Nxf6 11. b4 Be7 12. Qb3 O-O { This is another very comfortable position for black. }) 4. Bh4 Qb6 5. b3 Bf5 6. e3 Nd7 7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. Qxd3 e6 9. O-O Ne7 { We can't bring the knight to f6 here, on account of 9. Bxf6. } 10. c4 Nf5 11. Rc1 { If Bg3 then simply Nxg3 with total equality. } 11... Nxh4 12. Nxh4 { This just seems positionally very good for black. } 12... Be7 13. Nf3 O-O { There is absolutely no developed theory for this line, but this is one possible set of moves, which results in a more than decent position for black. }) (2. Nf3 { Also possible is to play the Colle System. This is a system opening for white, but black should gain easy equality. It is hardly ever played, but is nonetheless worth covering. } 2... d5 3. e3 Nf6 { Bf5 can also be played here, but this is rarely my preference. } 4. Bd3 Bg4 { However, there is no reason not to pin the knight here. } 5. Nbd2 { 5. c4 is slight better than the text move here, but that wouldn't be the Colle System! } 5... Nbd7 6. O-O e5 { Black is fine here after e6, which is also covered below. Nonetheless, e5 is a little more uncompromising. } (6... e6 7. c4 Be7 { Bd6 is sharper, but this move is granite and affords white few tactical opportunities. } 8. b3 O-O 9. Bb2 Bf5 10. Qc2 Bxd3 11. Qxd3 { This is absolutely fine for black, but the e5 line above gives black a slight edge with absolutely no risk or tension. In this line, at least black's dark-squared bishop is promising. Nonetheless, black has nothing to fear here. }) 7. dxe5 Nxe5 8. Be2 Bxf3 9. Bxf3 Nxf3+ 10. Nxf3 Bd6 11. b3 O-O { Black has achieved total equality. }) 2... d5 { 2...Nf6 is equally logical, but let's stick to our standard second move. } 3. Bg2 Nf6 4. Nf3 Bf5 { We can put the bishop outside of the pawn chain, as there is no Qb3 threat. } 5. O-O e6 { This has been something of a feeble opening, but the advantage for our opponent is that white has a veritable smorgasbord of possibilities here, each of which are equally valid.
So let's go over each of them briefly. } 6. Nh4 { White's big threat in many of these positions is Nh4. After this, black can't go back to g6, as Nxg6 weakens the kingside too much, and we have no compensation from the open h-file thanks to the white structure on the same side of the board. } (6. Nbd2 h6 7. Ne5 Bd6 { Black has tonnes of natural moves here. And they're all good! } 8. Re1 O-O { Easy-peasy equality! }) (6. Nc3 h6 7. Nd2 { Preparing e4, but black doesn't need to fear this. } 7... Be7 8. e4 dxe4 9. Ndxe4 Nbd7 10. Bf4 O-O { White has achieved nothing in the opening, other than surrendering the advantage of the white pieces! }) (6. c4 h6 7. Nc3 Be7 8. Re1 O-O 9. Qb3 Qb6 10. Qxb6 axb6 { Black is better here; the opening of the a-file is in our favour. }) 6... Bg4 { Instead, Bg4 is obligatory. } 7. c4 dxc4 { Black can safely capture here. } 8. h3 Bh5 9. g4 Bg6 10. Nxg6 hxg6 { This isn't as disadvantageous for black now, thanks to the weakening of the white kingside. } 11. e3 Nbd7 12. Qe2 Nb6 { Black can attempt to cling on to the c4-pawn here, although it is likely to fall eventually. } 13. Nd2 Bd6 { Nfd5 is also a strong manoeuvre. } 14. e4 Be7 { Moving the bishop back and forth appears nonsensical, but prompting e4 offers long-term promise for white, as this centre is visually appealing yet brittle. } 15. Nf3 c5 { And it can be challenged immediately... } 16. dxc5 Bxc5 17. Bf4 O-O { Black has a very comfortable game here, and is still a pawn up. } *

[Event "Complete Black c6 Repertoire: Example d4 Games"]
