{ As the d4 chapters are rather dense, I haven't included any example games, so here are a few. }
1. d4 c6 2. c4 (2. Nf3 { In a game with a 2192-rated opponent, white proffered a different move order, but the position soon transposed into familiar territory. } 2... d5 3. c4 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Qc2 Be7 { This was something of a weedy move. Better is Bd6. } 7. Bd3 h6 { For some reason, I opted for a very cautious opening scheme here, perhaps out of respect for my (at the time) higher rated opponent. } 8. Bd2 b6 9. O-O-O { It is highly unusual for white to castle queenside here, or in this opening generally, and also utterly unjustified. This just handed black an advantage that my play certainly hadn't earned. } 9... Bb7 10. e4 dxe4 11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. Bxe4 Nf6 { This theme of capturing on e4, and then gaining a tempo on the piece situated on the square, is often promising for black. Here the computer displays mild froideur for my rudimentary play, and declares Qc7 to be better. But black is perfectly fine here. } 13. Bd3 O-O 14. Bc3 { This achieves little of note, and with my kingside position secure, black can now begin a queenside attack. } 14... b5 15. b3 { This simply opens up white's already vulnerable position. c5 was essential. } 15... bxc4 16. Bxc4 c5 { This appears an unwise move, with dxc5 unleashing a discovered attack on the queen. But, in reality, black can just put the queen on c7, and enjoy a promising attack. } 17. dxc5 Qc7 18. b4 { White's position is teetering on the brink of collapse. } 18... a5 { Not particularly difficult to find this idea. } 19. a3 axb4 20. axb4 Ra7 { This isn't one of the best moves in the position, but I wanted to double rooks on the a-file. In fact, black's position is so superior that almost any sensible plan works. } 21. Kb2 Rfa8 { Taking on c5 here would have ended the game, but black is still much better. } 22. h4 { White has never really generated any counterplay in this game, and this token pawn push is too little far too late! } 22... Be4 23. Qd2 Qb7 { Almost anything is winning for white now. Again Bxc5 was lethal in this position. } 24. Qd4 Ra4 25. Bb3 Ra3 26. Ra1 Rxb3+ { Putting the queen on the a-file was even stronger, but black's king position is now woeful. } 27. Kxb3 Rd8 28. Qc4 { White had to find the tricky c6 to have any hopes of survival. This move represents virtual surrender. } 28... Bd5 { White threw in the towel. You can replay this game here: https://lichess.org/VWTi20el. Lesson learned: don't castle queenside as white! }) 2... d5 3. Nf3 (3. Nc3 { In another game against a 2191-rated opponent, black utilised an atypical move order with Nc3 here. } 3... Nf6 4. Bg5 { This approach is akin to the Trompowsky Attack. As this is rarely played, I didn't respond the best way to this sub-par move. } (4. Nf3 e6 5. Bg5 { In another game against a 2238-rated opponent, white deviated here with this pin-based opening approach. Black should definitely take on c4 here, but my move is more befitting of my style, and certainly doesn't hand white a significant advantage. } 5... Nbd7 6. e3 (6. cxd5 { In another game that I played with a 2348-rated opponent, this pawn exchange was ventured. } 6... cxd5 { Stockfish much prefers not blocking the bishop in here with exd5. But I included this game to show that sometimes you can go for a structure that you know, rather than what is objectively best. I believed that c-file pressure and control of the c4 square would compensate for my poorly placed bishop. } 7. e3 a6 { Bd6 was more principled, but I wanted to restrict my opponent's use of the b5-square. } 8. Bd3 Bd6 9. Bh4 { This was a curious move, and frankly a complete waste of a tempo. } 9... O-O { Black has largely equalised here, although white arguably has a slight positional edge. Certainly, I will have to demonstrate that my light-squared bishop isn't a significant disadvantage. } 10. O-O b5 { This is Stockfish's top choice as well. Black prepares to take control of the key c4-square. } 11. a3 Nb6 { There are many possible moves here, but I opted for the most direct plan. However, Stockfish's chilly verdict is that white is somewhat better after 12. e4 in this position, possessing both a stronger centre and marginally better piece placement. } 12. Rc1 Nc4 { This is a pesky knight. It prevents the white knight coming to the natural e5, and seems to potentially threaten both of white's queenside pawns (although this is illusory, as I will demonstrate in a sideline). } 13. a4 { Here white blundered badly. My opponent perhaps though that the a-pawn was hanging after 13. b3, but white could have achieved massive positional compensation, effectively neutering white's pieces. } (13. b3 Nxa3 14. Ne5 Bb7 15. f4 Be7 { Black is surviving here, and does have an extra pawn, but my position looks pretty awkward. My bishop on b7 is blocked in, my knight on a3 has nowhere to go, and white's pawns are coming on the kingside. This might be okay for black, but it's much easier for white to play. }) 13... Nxb2 { This knight b2 manoeuvre is always one to look out for when you have cemented the knight on b2. It is often possible, and frequently forks the bishop and queen, often to your advantage. Here it wins a pawn as well. } 14. Bxh7+ { My opponent resorted to this attempted swindle here. When you're in such a position, it's important to stay calm, calculate, and try to work out whether their idea works. } 14... Nxh7 { This seemed fine, as the white queen is hanging, with no feasible checks on the black king available. } 15. Qc2 { Nice intermezzo idea, but it doesn't work. } 15... Be7 { Unfortunately, I didn't find the best idea here. After 15...g5, black retains its extra piece. However, the next few moves are virtually forced, and black emerges much better. } 16. Bxe7 Qxe7 17. Qxb2 b4 18. Ne2 a5 { An experienced player will see immediately that black is much better here, and white must struggle in order to save the game. Even black's light-squared bishop is no longer a liability, as it can be used to attack the weak a4-pawn. } 19. Rc5 Nf6 20. Ng3 Bd7 21. Qb3 { I didn't really notice at the time, but this is a strange move that achieves nothing whatsoever. Not a tempo that white can afford to squander in this position. } 21... Rfc8 22. Rfc1 Qe8 { I was quite pleased with this backward queen move, which allows me to further pressure the a4-pawn, while also protecting my back rank. White's position is hanging by a thread now. } 23. Rxc8 { White panicked here, whereas simply protecting the a-pawn with Ra1 is much better. This should just be winning for black now, as you will see in the game. } 23... Rxc8 24. Rxc8 { Even here, although black is much better, Ra1 was a considerably more sensible move. Black has total control of the position now, with the two white knights stranded. My queen and bishop can merrily gang up on the a-pawn at their leisure. } 24... Qxc8 25. h3 { Stockfish claims this is the fourth best move in the position, but it looks unbelievably slow and ineffectual to me. } 25... Qc6 26. Ne5 Qxa4 { Important to double-check such moves, but nothing is hanging here. } 27. Qb2 { White's only hope is to retain its queen and play for a counter-attack on the kingside. The connected passers on a5 and b4 are impossible to stop, barring a massive error from black. } 27... Qd1+ { Not the best move, but I just threw in a safe check under time pressure. } 28. Kh2 Qa4 29. Qc1 Qb5 30. Qc7 a4 { I had to conduct some calculation here, but it seemed that there were no dangerous checks for black. However, 31. Nh5 is an excellent try in this position, and gives white some very dangerous counterplay. But this is hugely difficult to spot in a blitz game. } 31. Qd8+ Be8 { All of black's pieces are protecting one another, and white can simply resign here. } 32. f4 b3 33. f5 b2 34. fxe6 fxe6 35. Qe7 b1=Q 36. Qxe6+ Kh7 { Unfortunately, from white's perspective, the king is absolutely safe here. White doesn't even have any checks - neither knight can check the king, and my new queen is covering the b1-h7 diagonal. } 37. Qf5+ Qxf5 38. Nxf5 { My opponent tried a check anyway, and then resigned. Quite a decent game, and one that again illustrates the importance of black's queenside play in these lines. }) 6... Be7 { h6 was Stockfish's choice here, and is a thematic idea. } 7. Be2 dxc4 8. Bxc4 b5 9. Bb3 a6 10. O-O Bb7 { This remains my preferred structure in these lines. Black will seek to diminish the white centre via c5, animating my b7 bishop in the process. } 11. Rc1 Rc8 12. Qe2 O-O 13. Rfd1 c5 14. d5 c4 { This pawn push can be beneficial when the conditions support it. } 15. dxe6 { [%cal Gf7f6] } 15... fxe6 { Black cannot afford to take on b3 here; exd7 is way too strong. } 16. Bc2 b4 { Attempting to emphasise the power of my queenside pawns. } 17. Nb1 { White should have taken on f6 and then played Ne4. Now black is better. } 17... Qc7 { Centralising the knight was much stronger here. Now white can gain a tempo with Bf4. } 18. Nbd2 c3 19. bxc3 bxc3 { Black's passed pawn is slightly troublesome, but far too thoroughly barricaded to be dangerous at this point. } 20. Nf1 Ne5 21. Nxe5 Qxe5 22. Bf4 Qb5 23. Qxb5 { This was a particularly poor and anti-positional choice from my opponent. Black's b-pawn can now connect with pawn on c3, and I should be considerably better. } 23... axb5 24. Bb3 Bd5 25. Be5 Bxb3 26. axb3 b4 { Already at this point, I was planning an a-file invasion. } 27. Rc2 Ra8 28. Ng3 Ra3 29. Rb1 Rfa8 30. Kf1 Ra2 31. Rbc1 Rxc2 32. Rxc2 Ra1+ 33. Ke2 Rb1 { Black's play is supremely straightforward and natural. } 34. Ne4 { White was confronted with a difficult and depressing defence, and thus it wasn't that surprising that my opponent blundered, ending the game. } 34... Nxe4 { A nice positional game from my side, again emphasising that these d4 duels can be settled on the queenside, before white deploys any kingside play. You can replay this game here: https://lichess.org/JbAyormn/black#68 }) 4... e6 5. e4 { White is playing hyper-aggressive chess, but this approach isn't really justified. } 5... dxe4 6. Nxe4 Nbd7 { Bb4+ was better. } 7. Nf3 Be7 8. Bd3 Nxe4 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Bxe4 Nf6 { I missed a trick here, ignoring the dangerous Qb4+. } 11. Qd3 Nxe4 12. Qxe4 Qb4+ { Nonetheless, white was keen for me to play it! Black is much better now, with the white queen misplaced, } 13. Nd2 Qxb2 { Note that the b7 pawn is protected, so Rb1 doesn't really work here. Furthermore, this move hangs the a-pawn, and although white would have some compensation from tempi gained and black's boxed-in bishop, it's not enough to justify jettisoning two pawns. } 14. Ke2 { This quirky self-pinning king move is actually the best choice in the position. Rhb1 is a minor threat now. } 14... Qb6 { Not the best move according to Stockfish, but I wanted my king to have easy exit squares from the b-file. } 15. Rab1 Qc7 16. f4 b6 17. Nf3 Bb7 { Ba6 was perhaps a little stronger here, pinning the c-pawn. } 18. c5 Ba6+ 19. Kf2 bxc5 { A poor move positionally, weakening black's pawn structure and creating an open file for the rook on b1. Nonetheless, black is still a pawn up and much better. } 20. dxc5 O-O { Black finally castles on move 20. } 21. Ne5 Rac8 { Better was to occupy the yawning d-file. } 22. Rhd1 Rfd8 23. Rxd8+ { White could have equalised here with Qxc6. } 23... Qxd8 { This move now retains the c6-pawn, as Qc7 is strong after Nxc6. } 24. Nxc6 Qd2+ { However, with the white king still misplaced, I instead chose this unsound attack. } 25. Kg3 { This was an extremely strange choice, making white's task of saving the game far more difficult. } 25... Kh8 { Qc3+ is much stronger, but white still faces a highly tricky defence with the queens still on the board. } 26. Ne5 Kg8 27. Re1 Rxc5 28. Qa8+ Rc8 { I had already seen the retreat when snatching the c-pawn. } 29. Qxa7 { White blunders now. The only move was Qe4. } 29... Qxe1+ { In time pressure, we both blundered in the remainder of the game, but I managed to win the game eventually. Thematically, the importance of managing the central pawns is key. Replay the game here: https://lichess.org/bM4u63SN }) 3... Nf6 4. e3 e6 { I'm attempting to set up the Slav positions against my 2168-rated opponent. } 5. b3 { But my opponent errs from the typical with this interesting fianchetto concept. } 5... Nbd7 6. Bd3 b6 { Stockfish wants black to probe white's dark-squared weaknesses here with Bb4+, but my move isn't a bad second choice. } 7. O-O dxc4 { I wanted to take on c4 and gain a tempo on the bishop, but, of course, white isn't obliged to use the bishop to recapture. With this in mind, the c4 capture was a little rash and impatient. } 8. bxc4 Bb7 9. Bb2 c5 { I was hoping for central counterplay here with the customary c5 break. } 10. Nbd2 { Black was significantly better after d5, and my opponent has largely squandered the advantage now. } 10... Be7 11. Qe2 O-O { As is thematic, black's b7 bishop is the pride of my position. } 12. Rfd1 Qc7 13. Rac1 Rac8 { Occupying the c-file, as is de rigueur. } 14. h3 Rfe8 15. e4 { White at least stood equal before this move, which is a little impudent. Black can subvert white's centre now, and my opponent is soon worse. } 15... cxd4 16. e5 { Tempting, but just enables the f6-knight to find a pleasant square on f4. } 16... Nh5 17. Nxd4 Nf4 { Black is much better now, with the knight forking the queen and the g2 square. } 18. Qe3 Nxg2 19. Qg3 Qxe5 { This is strong, but perhaps even more pleasing in terms of aesthetics and the maximal deployment of forces was Bh4. } 20. Qg4 f5 { After Ne3, white was free to resign! The knight can't be taken because of Qxe3, after which my opponent's king is practically naked. Black is still much better after my move, but with some imagination white can somewhat muddy the waters. } 21. Qh5 { However, this was the wrong plan, and black's response is rather easy to find. } 21... Nf4 22. Ne2 { Under immense pressure, and with time running out, white blunders. This ended the game immediately, as black now wins a piece. } 22... Nxh5 23. Bxe5 Nxe5 { Not a perfect game, but an apt illustration of the power that the b7 bishop possesses when unleashed. } *

