1. c4 c6 2. d4 (2. Nf3 d5 3. d4 (3. e3 Nf6 4. d4 e6 5. Bd3 dxc4 { Another transpositional line. }) 3... Nf6 { A third transpositional line. }) (2. Nc3 d5 3. cxd5 cxd5 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. d4 Nc6 6. Bf4 a6 7. Qb3 e6 8. Rc1 Bd6 9. Bxd6 Qxd6 { This position is not strictly from the Queen's Gambit lines, but it should be comprehensible to all readers by now. }) (2. e3 d5 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Qc2 Nbd7 5. d4 e6 { It's actually very difficult for any opponent to avoid transposition here legitimately, unless white opts for a fianchetto line. }) 2... d5 { The most obvious way to transpose here is simply for black to immediately play 2. d4. Then we're in Queen's Gambit / Slav territory immediately. } *

