1. c4 c6 2. g3 (2. b3 d5 { 2. b3 is simply a lousy move. If white takes on d5, it cedes control of the centre to black. If not, black threatens to damage white's queenside pawns. } 3. Bb2 { A logical continuation following the queenside fianchetto, but allowing black to develop placidly. } 3... Bf5 4. e3 e6 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Nh4 { Slightly annoying, but not problematical. } 6... Bg4 7. Be2 Bxe2 8. Qxe2 Bd6 { Virtually any sensible move is fine for black. } 9. O-O O-O { White's b2 bishop is a good piece, but this is hardly a troubling position for black. }) 2... d5 3. Nf3 { White can't afford the immediate Bg2 here, as e5 gives black a big advantage. } 3... Nf6 4. Bg2 Bf5 5. O-O e6 { Even in this line, we've managed to erect the pawn triangle. } 6. d4 Be7 { Black shouldn't be unduly troubled by this line. } 7. Nh4 { This is probably the most unpleasant move in this position, as black can't afford the lackadaisical Bg6, and for its pawn structure to be ruined. } 7... Be4 { Thus, Be4 is good here. } 8. Nf3 (8. f3 { f3 looks good here... } 8... Bxb1 9. Rxb1 dxc4 { ...but black can simply capture twice! }) 8... h6 { Many options for black, but this is quite sound. } 9. Nc3 Bh7 { Black could also take on f3, but this seems far more natural. } 10. Bf4 O-O { Another solid position for black. } *

