[StudyName "Complete Black c6 Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "1. Nf3 – The Reti Opening"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iFA7tZcn/LKeXAZ2b"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/wub1234"]
[UTCDate "2021.01.05"]
[UTCTime "10:55:39"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. Nf3 { 1. Nf3 is another common opening choice for white, the third most popular overall, but one that offers very little that hasn't be observed elsewhere. White's potential to create original positions with this move is miniscule. } 1... c6 { Stockfish prefers 1...d5, but deems 1...c6 to be absolutely playable.
White now has several options, so let's again examine all of them. } 2. d4 (2. Nc3 d5 3. e4 dxe4 { This is the Caro-Kann Two Knights Variation... }) (2. e4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. d4 e6 { This is a line from the Caro-Kann Advance Variation... }) (2. e3 d5 3. c4 Nf6 4. Qc2 Nbd7 5. d4 { White is really struggling to avoid d4 here, but as soon as it is played... } 5... e6 { ...we're back in Queen's Gambit territory. }) (2. c4 Nf6 3. g3 d5 4. Bg2 e6 5. b3 Nbd7 6. Bb2 { This is an interesting setup, and one of the few ways that white can lure us out of book. } 6... Bd6 { Stockfish much prefers b5 here, but this move is easy to spot, and white has little advantage after the natural 7. d4, and none whatsoever after any other move. } 7. d4 O-O 8. O-O { This is a reasonable position for white. Black must play to undermine the queenside structure of his opponent, via b5, a5, etc. For example, play might continue as follows... } 8... b5 9. Nbd2 bxc4 10. bxc4 Rb8 11. Qc2 Ba6 12. Rab1 Bb4 { This is one of the harder positions to play of all that we have examined, and also one of the least stereotypical. But this demonstrates just how hard it is for white to create completely unfamiliar positions.
Also, it should be noted that Stockfish deems the two sides equal here. }) 2... d5 { 2. d4 is already drifting towards Queen's Gambit waters. 3. c4 is the obvious move here, after which we've already transposed, while Stockfish also recommends the lugubrious 3. a3 and 3. h3. } 3. c4 (3. h3 Bf5 4. Bf4 e6 { No threat of Qb3 here. } 5. e3 Bd6 6. Bxd6 Qxd6 7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. Qxd3 Nf6 { Again, this is a very familiar position. }) (3. a3 Nf6 4. Bf4 Bf5 5. e3 e6 6. Nbd2 Bd6 7. Bxd6 Qxd6 8. c4 Nbd7 9. Be2 O-O { One possible example line after 3. a3. Again, über familiar. }) 3... Nf6 4. e3 e6 { This is now completely transposed. } 5. Bd3 dxc4 6. Bxc4 b5 7. Bd3 a6 8. O-O Bb7 { This remains my favourite line, but many other Queen's Gambit system are available, other than the Slav, as you develop as a player. } *

[Event "Complete Black c6 Repertoire: 1. g3 – King's Indian Attack"]
[Date "2021.01.05"]
