[StudyName "Complete Black c6 Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "1. g3 – King's Indian Attack"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iFA7tZcn/tm6Hq9PC"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/wub1234"]
[UTCDate "2021.01.05"]
[UTCTime "10:55:49"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. g3 { g3 is rarely deployed nowadays; this hypermodern opening is deemed rather crude, with 1. Nf3 instead considered a necessary precursor to any fianchetto.
Thus, I have rarely faced this, but it's worthwhile to go over some of the possible variations associated with this move. } 1... c6 { 1...c6 isn't necessarily the best move here. Stockfish wants black to play for a Sicilian with c5. However, that requires learning an entirely new opening system! And 1...c6 leads to positions that are easily comprehensible for us by now, and perfectly sound.
So let's again examine all of white's valid moves. } 2. Bg2 { Logical is simply to develop the bishop. } (2. Nf3 d5 3. c4 Nf6 4. Bg2 dxc4 5. O-O Nbd7 6. Na3 b5 7. Nd4 a6 8. d3 Qc7 9. dxc4 { White can attempt to muddy the waters, but black still achieves equality, and even a slight edge. Bxc6 might appear appealing, but simply enables black to exchange off the light-squared bishop. } 9... Bb7 10. Bg5 c5 11. Bxb7 Qxb7 12. Nf3 e6 13. Qb3 b4 14. Bxf6 Nxf6 15. Nc2 Be7 16. Qa4+ Qd7 17. Qxd7+ Nxd7 18. Rfd1 Bf6 19. Rab1 Ke7 { Black is slightly better, and can hope for a superior endgame. }) (2. c4 d5 3. Nf3 { 3. Nf3 is essential here, as it prevents e5 from black. } 3... Nf6 4. Bg2 dxc4 { Again, this line promises nothing that we haven't witnessed before. }) (2. d4 d5 3. c4 Nf6 4. Bg2 Bf5 5. Qb3 Qb6 6. Qxb6 axb6 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Bd2 e6 9. e4 { This looks like a blunder from black, but we have a saving move. } 9... Nb4 10. exf5 Nc2+ 11. Ke2 Nxa1 { Black may have won the exchange, but white has massive compensation, so there is little advantage here. } 12. Nf3 Rxa2 13. Nc3 Ra8 14. fxe6 fxe6 15. Bh3 Nc2 16. Bxe6 Nd7 17. Bf4 Be7 18. h4 { There is a vast amount of play left here, but black is better, and can play for win. This is definitely one of the more complex lines that we have examined in this repertoire, and shows the depths that white has to plumb in order to create original positions (that are still worse for white!). }) 2... d5 { Nf6 allows 3. e4. } 3. Nf3 (3. d4 Nf6 4. Nf3 Bf5 5. O-O e6 6. Nh4 Bg4 { 3. d4 is possible here, but it tends to lead to similar lines. }) 3... Nf6 4. O-O (4. c4 dxc4 5. O-O Nbd7 6. Qc2 b5 7. b3 cxb3 8. Qxc6 Rb8 9. axb3 Bb7 10. Qc2 a6 11. d4 e6 12. Bd2 Rc8 13. Qb2 Bd6 14. Re1 O-O { If 4. c4, we can accept the gambit, and obtain a very decent position from the opening. Of course, there are other ideas available to white, but there shouldn't be anything too tricky for black here. }) 4... Bf5 5. d3 (5. Nh4 Be4 { The immediate 5. Nh4 is also playable here, after which black is happy to exchange on e4 if this is permitted. } 6. f3 Bg6 { Better though is to essay 6. f3, after which the bishop can comfortably return to g6. Nxg6 is not so strong here, as black gets unencumbered development. } 7. d3 e6 8. Nxg6 hxg6 9. Be3 c5 10. Nd2 Nc6 11. Bf2 Be7 12. c3 Qc7 { The direct 12...d4 is also strong here. But this more cautious move enables black to attain a comfortable position. } 13. d4 cxd4 14. cxd4 O-O { Black should be slightly wary of the h-file, but is otherwise absolutely fine. }) 5... e6 6. Nh4 { The Nh4 move is viable again here. Bg6 is more playable than in some of the other lines that we've assessed, but, once again, the following plan is more advisable. } (6. b3 Be7 7. Bb2 Nbd7 8. Re1 O-O { White can also attempt a double-fianchetto here, which may offer long-term possibilities, despite achieving little in the short-term. }) 6... Bg4 7. h3 Bh5 8. Nc3 Nbd7 9. g4 Bg6 (9... Nxg4 10. hxg4 Bxg4 11. Nf3 Bd6 { If you're feeling frisky then this sacrificial line is vaguely playable. White should be better here, but black has considerable compensation for the piece, and must now launch a kingside attack. }) 10. Nxg6 hxg6 11. e4 Bd6 12. exd5 (12. Re1 dxe4 13. g5 Nh5 14. Nxe4 Bf4 15. Be3 Qc7 16. c4 Bxe3 17. Rxe3 O-O { If 12. Re1 then white's central play doesn't really work, and black obtains a slight edge. The black knight can come to f4, and the black pieces appear better coordinated, although Stockfish believes that this is no big deal for the white side. }) 12... cxd5 13. Nb5 (13. Be3 Ne5 { 13. Be3 is awkward for black, and Stockfish actually recommends the strange looking 13...Kf8 here. But this line is far more natural, and easier to play. } 14. f4 Nc6 15. Nb5 Be7 16. c4 a6 17. Na3 { If 17. Nc3 then d4 wins a piece. } 17... O-O { White has a space advantage, black has the better pawn structure and coordination; a dynamically balanced position. }) 13... Bb8 { This looks awkward, but is a necessary precaution. } 14. Re1 (14. c3 O-O { 14. c3 is also playable here, but nothing about which to be unduly fretful. }) 14... a6 15. Nd4 Ba7 { Qc7 is also playable here, but it seems anti-positional to quarantine the rook on a8. } 16. Ne2 (16. Be3 O-O { Another option for white, but black still achieves equality. }) 16... Rc8 { Our favourite lateral rook move! } 17. Bf4 (17. c3 { 17. c3 is also playable here. } 17... O-O { But black can still castle with a degree of comfort. } 18. Bg5 Bb8 19. a4 Qc7 { Black has many options here, but terrorising the vulnerable dark squares of white seems an intriguing ploy. }) 17... O-O { Black has a decent position here. } *

[Event "Complete Black c6 Repertoire: 1. Nc3 – Dunst Opening"]
[Date "2021.01.05"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B18"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Classical Variation"]
[StudyName "Complete Black c6 Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "1. Nc3 – Dunst Opening"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iFA7tZcn/39EJrY6j"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/wub1234"]
[UTCDate "2021.01.05"]
[UTCTime "10:55:57"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. Nc3 { Another possibility for black as an opening foray is 1. Nc3. Although it may not be the most natural or principled choice, 1...c6 is still perfectly playable here. And, as we shall see, white will almost inevitably end up in positions with which we are ultra-familiar by now. } 1... c6 { Let's analyse all of white's feasible choices. } 2. d4 (2. Nf3 { Instead, white can play Nf3 here. } 2... d5 { 2...d5 follows anyway, and now white has a few possible choices. } 3. e4 (3. e3 Bf5 4. d4 e6 5. Bd3 Bxd3 6. Qxd3 Nf6 { We have now transposed into structures reminiscent of the Queen's Gambit, except white has blocked the c-pawn with the knight. }) (3. d4 Nf6 4. e3 Bf5 { Again, we're just heading for Queen's Gambit-type positions. White can stir things up by throwing in a g2 fianchetto (although this isn't positionally justified), but there is little our opponent can do that will surprise or disturb us. }) (3. h3 Bf5 { 3. h3 seems a rather timid move, but it results in similar structures once more. } 4. d4 e6 5. Bf4 Bd6 6. Bxd6 Qxd6 7. e3 Nd7 8. Bd3 Bxd3 9. Qxd3 Ngf6 10. O-O O-O { Black is absolutely fine here. }) (3. a3 Bf5 4. d4 e6 { 3. a3 is a waiting move, which once again enables us to plough a familiar furrow. } 5. Bf4 Bd6 6. Bxd6 Qxd6 { Already, black has equalised here. }) 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 { Now we're in the Two Knight Caro-Kann. }) (2. e4 { 2. e4 is also playable here. } 2... d5 { But 2...d5 follows regardless! Now white has several options again. } 3. d4 (3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 { This is the Two Knights Caro-Kann. }) (3. h3 e6 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. e5 Nfd7 6. d4 c5 { This is one of the trickier lines to play, where black cannot easily prevent e5. But this structure is quite reminiscent of the Advance Variation of the Caro-Kann, and is quite playable. } 7. Ne2 Nc6 8. c3 Be7 9. a3 O-O { Black has equalised, and will play for pawn expansions on the queenside; another strategy with which were supremely familiar. }) (3. Qf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 { This 3. Qf3 move is unusual but playable. However, we can set up a familiar black system quite easily. } 5. d4 Ngf6 6. c3 Nxe4 7. Qxe4 Nf6 { All of these moves should come very naturally. } 8. Qc2 Bg4 { An idea from the Exchange Variation of the Caro-Kann. } 9. Be2 Bxe2 10. Qxe2 e6 11. Nf3 Bd6 12. Bg5 h6 13. Bh4 { If Bxf6 here, black has completely equalised. } 13... O-O { The middlegame begins! }) 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 { Yes, we're in the Caro-Kann again! }) (2. a3 d5 { Any such early waiting moves invite 2...d5. Now we're almost inevitably going to get Caro-Kann or Queen's Gambit structures. } 3. d4 Nf6 { White must surely occupy the centre with its third move, and already the game is drifting towards recognisable territory. } 4. Bf4 Bf5 5. e3 e6 { Once again, black is fine here. }) (2. h3 d5 3. d4 Nf6 { 2. h3 leads to similar positions to 2. a3. } 4. Bf4 Nbd7 5. Nf3 e6 6. e3 a6 7. Bd3 c5 { There are other possible options for black over the last few moves, but these should all of the ideas and moves here should be reassuringly familiar. }) 2... d5 { If 2. d4 d5, Stockfish recommends 3. e4. } 3. e4 (3. e3 Bf5 { If 3. e3 then Bf5 leads to typical Queen's Gambit structures. } 4. Nf3 e6 5. Bd3 Bxd3 6. Qxd3 Nf6 7. a3 Nbd7 { Black is already perfectly fine here. }) (3. Bf4 Nf6 4. e3 Bf5 { After 3. Bf4, we're still heading towards familiar territory. }) (3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 Bg4 5. Bd3 Nbd7 6. O-O { Nf3 sees us sailing in familiar waters once more. Black is already slightly better here, and can even play the more aggressive e5. But e6, creating the pawn triangle, is absolutely fine as well. } 6... e6 7. h3 Bh5 8. e4 dxe4 9. Nxe4 Nxe4 10. Bxe4 Nf6 { Again, the same exchange and tempi trick on e4. } 11. Bd3 Bxf3 12. Qxf3 Qxd4 13. Rd1 Qd5 14. Qe2 O-O-O { Black can castle queenside here, and is slightly better. }) 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 { Now we're just in the mainline Caro-Kann. } 5. Ng3 Bg6 { In conclusion, it's tricky for white to find a critical line that forces us out of book with 1.Nc3, and we should be very comfortable playing 1...c6 as a response.
1...c6 is absolutely not forced. 1....d5 is probably the best move. But it's perfectly okay to play 1...c6, particularly as you will very rarely encounter this opening, and 1. Nc3 players will equally seldom face 1...c6. } *

[Event "Complete Black c6 Repertoire: 1. b3 – Larsen's Opening / Nimzo–Larsen Attack"]
[Date "2021.01.10"]
