[StudyName "Complete Black c6 Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "1. b3 – Larsen's Opening / Nimzo–Larsen Attack"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iFA7tZcn/ufT1Yc12"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/wub1234"]
[UTCDate "2021.01.10"]
[UTCTime "23:57:25"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. b3 { Finally, this peculiar move, pioneered by Bent Larsen, is actually quite playable. Larsen inspired Bobby Fischer to attempt 1. b3, and it has been proved to be okay for white. Nonetheless, I can't recall anyone ever playing this against me! But let's not allow that to deter us... } 1... c6 { Again, 1...c6 isn't the best move here, but it's very difficult for black to do anything to punish us, as we will see. I should emphasise that we're examining weird openings here, which hardly ever appear on the board. I haven't even properly learnt this opening; after all, I've never faced it! } 2. Bb2 d5 { 2...Nf6 is also playable here, but it makes practically no difference whatsoever which one black chooses. } 3. Nf3 { 3. Nf3 is natural. We will examine other moves here below. } (3. g3 { A twin-fianchetto may suit the tastes of those that dabble with this opening. But it shouldn't be considered intimidating. } 3... Nf6 4. Nf3 Bg4 { Bf5 is also just as good here. } 5. Bg2 e6 6. O-O Bd6 7. h3 Bh5 8. d3 O-O 9. Re1 Nbd7 { Again, black has achieved complete equality. }) (3. e3 Bf5 { The Queen's Gambit-esque 3. e3 is vaguely playable here, although it seems rather contrived and artificial. } 4. d4 e6 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Qxd3 Nbd7 8. Nbd2 Bd6 9. O-O O-O 10. e4 dxe4 11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. Qxe4 Nf6 13. Qe2 { Black's play will now focus, as is so often the case in this repertoire, on queenside expansion. }) (3. c4 { An instant 3. c4 is possibly feasible, but can be considered an ugly and deficient attempt at originality. } 3... Bf5 4. e3 e6 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. d4 Bd6 7. Nf3 O-O 8. Bd3 Nbd7 { We can definitely allow the doubling of pawns here with Bxf5, as black is ahead in development, and the pawn on f5 could later be used as a bargaining chip in a potential attack. } 9. O-O { However, it's better for white to simply castle, and accept that black is slightly better. }) 3... Nf6 4. c4 Bg4 { Black has other possible choices, but shifting the bishop outside of the pawn chain makes sense. } 5. e3 e6 { Creating the omnipresent pawn triangle! } 6. Nc3 Nbd7 { Black has already equalised here, and has a pleasant choice - this would be mine. } 7. Be2 Bd6 8. O-O O-O { White has garnered nothing of note from the opening thus far. Now several plans are possible. } 9. Rc1 Rc8 { Black has many valid choices, but occupying the c-file is absolutely fine. } 10. h3 { This is the best choice for white, but still leads to a completely equal position. } 10... Bh5 11. d4 Re8 12. Ne5 { It's logical for the knight to occupy the oft-valuable e5 square in this position. } 12... Bxe2 { Don't forget that the knight move delivers a discovered attack on the bishop! A very common tactic of which to beware! } 13. Nxe2 { If Qxe2 then black simply wins a pawn by taking on e5. } 13... Qc7 { Black has completed development and has a rock solid position here. } *

